window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2022-12-2", "source": "2022年12月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence “In an era of information explosion, it is vitally important to develop the ability t o think critically and make rational choices.” You can make statements, give reasons, or cite examples to develop your essay.You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "In an era of information explosion, it is vitally important to develop the ability to think critically and make\nrational choices. Every day we are flooded with news, advertisements and social-media posts, yet not all of\nthem are trustworthy. Only those who can sift truth from falsehood are able to stay clear-headed.\nThere are two compelling reasons why this ability matters. First, information today is a mixture of facts and\nrumours, and a careless reader can easily be misled. Take online rumours during public emergencies for\nexample: people who forward them without checking often spread panic. Second, the cost of a wrong choice\nhas risen sharply; whether we are selecting a course, a product or a career, rational judgement based on reliable\nevidence saves us from regret.\nTherefore, we should never swallow information passively. By questioning the source, comparing different\nviews and weighing the evidence before us, we can turn the endless flood of information into a real advantage\nrather than a heavy burden. In the end, critical thinking is not an optional skill but a survival tool for the\nmodern mind."}, "translation": {"prompt": "黄土高原( the Loess Plateau)是中国第三大高原， 面积约60万平方公里，平均海拔 1000-2000米，绝大部分覆盖着50-80米厚的黄土，是世界上黄土分布最集中、覆盖厚度最大的区域。这是大自然创造的一个奇迹，在世界上也是绝无仅有的。 黄土高原是中华民族的发祥地之一。早在5500年前，人们就已经在黄土高原上开始农耕。随着农耕业的持续发展，黄土高原人口不断增加，在秦汉时期就成为中国的政治和经济中心。如今，随着西部大开发战略的实施，黄土高原地区的经济得到了迅速发展。", "sample": "The Loess Plateau, China's third largest plateau, has an area of about 600,000 square kilometers and an average altitude of 1,000 to 2,000 meters, with most of it covered by loess 50 to 80 meters thick; it is the region with the most concentrated distribution and the greatest thickness of loess in the world. It is a miracle created by nature and is unique in the world. The Loess Plateau is one of the birthplaces of the Chinese nation. As early as 5,500 years ago, people had already begun farming on the Loess Plateau. With the continuous development of farming, its population kept growing, and during the Qin and Han dynasties it became the political and economic center of China. Today, with the implementation of the Western Development Strategy, the economy of the Loess Plateau region has developed rapidly."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2022-12-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "The task of the global strategist of a business is to build a platform of capabilities derived from the resources, experiences and innovations of units operating in multiple locations, to transplant those capabilities wherever  __26__  , and then to systematically upgrade and renew them—ahead of the competition.\nApple is an outstanding case of a company whose unique capabilities give it a worldwide  __27__  advantage, particularly with respect to its ability to build platforms from a product base that integrates functional and  __28__  design. Apple has been able to leverage and exploit its California-based design and marketing advantages successfully throughout the world. IKEA is another such case. The do-it-yourself furniture and houseware company first developed a compelling set of capabilities to design, manufacture and  __29__  furniture at low cost and sell it in a novel way in Sweden. Later, IKEA successfully  __30__  this formula in many other countries.\nBy contrast, Telefonica, a Spanish telecommunications company that is now the world's fifth largest telecom by  __31__  , first developed its special advantage abroad. In 1989 and 1990, Telefonica had the opportunity to enter Chile and Argentina, countries that shared many institutional and cultural characteristics with its home country but that were  __32__  more rapid market reform. Throughout the 1990s, Telefonica took what it learned in Chile and Argentina about reconstructing former state-owned telecoms to other Latin American countries that were privatizing their state telecoms and deregulating their telecom markets.\nThese examples might lead the reader to believe that creating a global advantage is an easy task. But many other  __33__  of expensive failed experiments suggest that creating a lasting global advantage actually requires a great deal of  __34__  and operational finesse(技巧). Our research suggests that global winners typically create and sustain their international presence through a systematic process of  __35__  , renewing and enhancing their core capabilities.", "matchIntro": "Fear of Nature: An Emerging Threat to Conservation", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "What do we lose when natural spaces and species disappear? Increasingly, research has shown that as species and ecosystems vanish, it also chips away at our ability to preserve what remains—because we no longer understand what we're losing."}, {"label": "B", "text": "You probably see it all the time. The neighbor who puts pesticides on his lawn rather than deal with annoying bees. The politician who votes against wildlife protection because she's never seen a wolf in the wild. The corporation that wants to bulldoze(用推土机推平) the habitat of a rare frog."}, {"label": "C", "text": "At best this can be termed \"the extinction of experience,\" where our cultural and natural histories fade from our memories and therefore our reality. At its worst it becomes something even more concerning: \"biophobia,\" the fear of living things and a complete aversion to nature."}, {"label": "D", "text": "This isn't the fiction of living in a cold, empty dystopia(绝望的世界). Sadly, it's becoming a way of life for too many people—especially children. A recent study in Japan paints a striking portrait of this problem. A survey of more than 5,300 school children in the Tochigi Prefecture examined their perception of 14 local insect species and one spider. The results? A collective \"ew!\" Most of the students saw the species as things to dislike or fear, or even as sources of danger. The less experience the students had with nature, the more negative their feelings."}, {"label": "E", "text": "The results were published earlier this year in the journal Biological Conservation. Lead researcher Masashi Soga with the University of Tokyo says the study stemmed from observations about today's nature-deficient children. \"Humans inherently avoid dangerous organisms such as bees, but children these days avoid even harmless insects such as butterflies and dragonflies(蜻蜓),\" he says. \"I have long wondered why so many of today's children react like this.\""}, {"label": "F", "text": "Although the children's reactions were somewhat expected, the new study did contain an unexpected finding: Many of the surveyed children revealed that their parents also expressed fear or disgust of the same animals. In fact, these parental emotions were strong enough to overwhelm any positive experiences the children might have gained from direct experiences in nature. As Soga and his coauthors wrote in their paper, \"Our results suggest that there is likely a feedback loop in which an increase in people who have negative attitudes towards nature in one generation will lead to a further increase in people with similar attitudes in the next generation.\""}, {"label": "G", "text": "And that's possibly the greater threat posed by extinction of experience. Soga suggests the generational loss—condition previously dubbed environmental generational amnesia(遗忘)—could chip away at our societal ability to preserve what we're losing. \"I believe that increased biophobia is a major, but invisible, threat to global biodiversity,\" Soga says. \"As the number of children who have biophobia increases, public interest and support for biodiversity conservation will gradually decline. Although many conservation biologists still consider that preventing the loss of wildlife habitat is the most important way to conserve biodiversity, I think preventing increased biophobia is also important for conservation.\""}, {"label": "H", "text": "What's to be done about this? The paper makes several recommendations, the most obvious of which is that children should experience nature more often. The authors also suggest establishing policies to guide these natural experiences and increasing educational programs about the natural world."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Helping parents to see species around them in a new light would make a difference, too. And, of course, maintaining support for preserving the wild spaces where these \"scary\" creatures live is the most important thing of all. That's a point reinforced by another recent study, which found that wild spaces located within urban areas—and the plants and animals that thrive in them—are particularly important for human health and well-being."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Published in the journal Frontiers in Sustainable Cities, the study examined attitudes toward Discovery Park, the heavily forested 534-acre public park in Seattle, Washington. It found that the public had the most appreciation for—and gained the most value from—the wildest parts of the park. \"I have seen whales, seals, fish, eagles, shorebirds and many other sea creatures in their natural habitat,\" one survey participant wrote. \"Coming here with people has allowed me to connect and talk with them about conversation that simply does not happen in everyday life,\" wrote another."}, {"label": "K", "text": "The participants reported that their most valuable experiences in the park included encountering wildlife, walking through open spaces, exploring the beach and finding beautiful views. \"We saw that a large majority of participants' interactions, especially their most meaningful interactions, depended on Discovery Park's relative wildness,\" says lead author Elizabeth Lev, a master's student in the University of Washington's Human Interaction with Nature Lab. This is only possible because the park is relatively wild. After all, you can't enjoy watching birds if there are no birds to follow, gaze at the sunset if it's obscured by skyscrapers, or stop and smell the flowers if they don't have room to grow."}, {"label": "L", "text": "And yet even this long-protected space could someday become less hospitable to nature. Over the past few years a lot of people and organizations have suggested developing parts of Discovery Park or the neighboring area. Most recently a plan proposed building 34 acres of much-needed affordable housing and parking spaces adjacent to the park, bringing with them noise, traffic and pollution."}, {"label": "M", "text": "If anything like that happened, both the park and the people of Seattle could lose something vital. And that would continue the trend of chipping away at Seattle's—and the world's—natural spaces, leaving just tiny pocket parks and green-but-empty spaces that offer little real value to wildlife, plants or people."}, {"label": "N", "text": "\"It is true that any interaction with nature is better than none, but I don't want people to be satisfied with any small bit of grass and trees,\" Lev says. \"We have been in this cycle of environmental generational amnesia for a long time, where the baseline keeps shifting and we don't even realize what we're losing until it's gone. If we can get people to understand how much meaning and value can come from having more experiences with more wild forms of nature, then maybe we can stop this cycle and move toward conserving and restoring what we have left.\""}, {"label": "O", "text": "Building this understanding in an ever-more fearful and disconnected world may be the biggest challenge. Peter Kahn, the senior author of Lev's paper and the director of the Human Interaction with Nature lab, made several suggestions for bridging this gap in this 2011 book, Technological Nature. They echo the recommendation about getting children into nature, but also include telling stories of how things used to be, imagining what things might be like in the future, and developing a common language about nature, \"a way of speaking about wild and domestic interaction patterns, and the meaningful, deep and often joyful feelings that they generate.\""}, {"label": "P", "text": "No matter what techniques we use, this growing field of research illustrates that saving nature requires encouraging people to experience it more often and more deeply. That calls for additional research—Lev and her coauthors have published a toolkit that other municipalities can follow to study the value of their own wild spaces—and clear communication of the results. \"If we can continue to show people the benefits of these wild spaces,\" Lev says, \"maybe people will begin to see more value in keeping these areas undeveloped—for the sake of our mutual benefit.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Some people in the US have asserted that forgiving student loan debt is one way to stimulate the economy and give assistance to those in need. One government proposition is to eliminate $10,000 of debt for 'economically distressed' students. Some in US Congress have gone so far as to suggest forgiving up to $50,000 in debt per student borrower, but does forgiving student debt necessarily correlate to helping the economically disadvantaged?\nThe answer is no. This policy is just giving money away to universities and the most affluent students in attendance. Federal Reserve data reveals that the highest-income 40 percent of households owe approximately 60 percent of outstanding student debt, while the lowest 40 percent owe just under 20 percent. This could be due to a combination of factors: students from high-income households are more likely to go to expensive colleges, less likely to receive financial aid, and more likely to have high incomes post-graduation. Plus, the majority of student debt is held by graduate degree earners, who earn approximately 25 percent more than their undergraduate counterparts. Clearly, giving free reign to banks to forgive student debt is a step in the wrong direction.\nOther proposals for broader, long-term student loan plans have some fundamental problems. One idea is to cancel student debt only for undergraduate degrees and for students making less than $125,000.\nThis attempts to address the fact that Congress' previously mentioned student loan forgiveness plan largely helps out the wealthy, but is an adverse incentive for universities to keep raising tuition and for students to choose to major in low-earning degree programs. Colleges have no reason to make their programs more affordable if they believe students will just take out more debt. And, students will feel more comfortable making the irresponsible decision to go tens of thousands of dollars in debt to major in impractical or idealistic subjects if they know their loans will be forgiven.\nThis is especially concerning given the pandemic(大流行病) has rendered a college education practically worthless. Students are paying tens of thousands of dollars per year to live at home and be lectured on the Internet. Do we really want to tell colleges that they can get away with providing below-average service for an outrageous cost?\nIn the case of any of these student debt plans, working-class Americans who chose not to or could not afford to go to college will be subsidizing the education of the professional class. Plumbers and retail workers will be paying for the degrees of doctors and lawyers.\nThe US government's effort to help those in debt is commendable but is this really the solution that will help the poor financially recover?", "47": "Some people in the US have asserted that forgiving student loan debt is one way to stimulate the economy and give assistance to those in need. One government proposition is to eliminate $10,000 of debt for 'economically distressed' students. Some in US Congress have gone so far as to suggest forgiving up to $50,000 in debt per student borrower, but does forgiving student debt necessarily correlate to helping the economically disadvantaged?\nThe answer is no. This policy is just giving money away to universities and the most affluent students in attendance. Federal Reserve data reveals that the highest-income 40 percent of households owe approximately 60 percent of outstanding student debt, while the lowest 40 percent owe just under 20 percent. This could be due to a combination of factors: students from high-income households are more likely to go to expensive colleges, less likely to receive financial aid, and more likely to have high incomes post-graduation. Plus, the majority of student debt is held by graduate degree earners, who earn approximately 25 percent more than their undergraduate counterparts. Clearly, giving free reign to banks to forgive student debt is a step in the wrong direction.\nOther proposals for broader, long-term student loan plans have some fundamental problems. One idea is to cancel student debt only for undergraduate degrees and for students making less than $125,000.\nThis attempts to address the fact that Congress' previously mentioned student loan forgiveness plan largely helps out the wealthy, but is an adverse incentive for universities to keep raising tuition and for students to choose to major in low-earning degree programs. Colleges have no reason to make their programs more affordable if they believe students will just take out more debt. And, students will feel more comfortable making the irresponsible decision to go tens of thousands of dollars in debt to major in impractical or idealistic subjects if they know their loans will be forgiven.\nThis is especially concerning given the pandemic(大流行病) has rendered a college education practically worthless. Students are paying tens of thousands of dollars per year to live at home and be lectured on the Internet. Do we really want to tell colleges that they can get away with providing below-average service for an outrageous cost?\nIn the case of any of these student debt plans, working-class Americans who chose not to or could not afford to go to college will be subsidizing the education of the professional class. Plumbers and retail workers will be paying for the degrees of doctors and lawyers.\nThe US government's effort to help those in debt is commendable but is this really the solution that will help the poor financially recover?", "48": "Some people in the US have asserted that forgiving student loan debt is one way to stimulate the economy and give assistance to those in need. One government proposition is to eliminate $10,000 of debt for 'economically distressed' students. Some in US Congress have gone so far as to suggest forgiving up to $50,000 in debt per student borrower, but does forgiving student debt necessarily correlate to helping the economically disadvantaged?\nThe answer is no. This policy is just giving money away to universities and the most affluent students in attendance. Federal Reserve data reveals that the highest-income 40 percent of households owe approximately 60 percent of outstanding student debt, while the lowest 40 percent owe just under 20 percent. This could be due to a combination of factors: students from high-income households are more likely to go to expensive colleges, less likely to receive financial aid, and more likely to have high incomes post-graduation. Plus, the majority of student debt is held by graduate degree earners, who earn approximately 25 percent more than their undergraduate counterparts. Clearly, giving free reign to banks to forgive student debt is a step in the wrong direction.\nOther proposals for broader, long-term student loan plans have some fundamental problems. One idea is to cancel student debt only for undergraduate degrees and for students making less than $125,000.\nThis attempts to address the fact that Congress' previously mentioned student loan forgiveness plan largely helps out the wealthy, but is an adverse incentive for universities to keep raising tuition and for students to choose to major in low-earning degree programs. Colleges have no reason to make their programs more affordable if they believe students will just take out more debt. And, students will feel more comfortable making the irresponsible decision to go tens of thousands of dollars in debt to major in impractical or idealistic subjects if they know their loans will be forgiven.\nThis is especially concerning given the pandemic(大流行病) has rendered a college education practically worthless. Students are paying tens of thousands of dollars per year to live at home and be lectured on the Internet. Do we really want to tell colleges that they can get away with providing below-average service for an outrageous cost?\nIn the case of any of these student debt plans, working-class Americans who chose not to or could not afford to go to college will be subsidizing the education of the professional class. Plumbers and retail workers will be paying for the degrees of doctors and lawyers.\nThe US government's effort to help those in debt is commendable but is this really the solution that will help the poor financially recover?", "49": "Some people in the US have asserted that forgiving student loan debt is one way to stimulate the economy and give assistance to those in need. One government proposition is to eliminate $10,000 of debt for 'economically distressed' students. Some in US Congress have gone so far as to suggest forgiving up to $50,000 in debt per student borrower, but does forgiving student debt necessarily correlate to helping the economically disadvantaged?\nThe answer is no. This policy is just giving money away to universities and the most affluent students in attendance. Federal Reserve data reveals that the highest-income 40 percent of households owe approximately 60 percent of outstanding student debt, while the lowest 40 percent owe just under 20 percent. This could be due to a combination of factors: students from high-income households are more likely to go to expensive colleges, less likely to receive financial aid, and more likely to have high incomes post-graduation. Plus, the majority of student debt is held by graduate degree earners, who earn approximately 25 percent more than their undergraduate counterparts. Clearly, giving free reign to banks to forgive student debt is a step in the wrong direction.\nOther proposals for broader, long-term student loan plans have some fundamental problems. One idea is to cancel student debt only for undergraduate degrees and for students making less than $125,000.\nThis attempts to address the fact that Congress' previously mentioned student loan forgiveness plan largely helps out the wealthy, but is an adverse incentive for universities to keep raising tuition and for students to choose to major in low-earning degree programs. Colleges have no reason to make their programs more affordable if they believe students will just take out more debt. And, students will feel more comfortable making the irresponsible decision to go tens of thousands of dollars in debt to major in impractical or idealistic subjects if they know their loans will be forgiven.\nThis is especially concerning given the pandemic(大流行病) has rendered a college education practically worthless. Students are paying tens of thousands of dollars per year to live at home and be lectured on the Internet. Do we really want to tell colleges that they can get away with providing below-average service for an outrageous cost?\nIn the case of any of these student debt plans, working-class Americans who chose not to or could not afford to go to college will be subsidizing the education of the professional class. Plumbers and retail workers will be paying for the degrees of doctors and lawyers.\nThe US government's effort to help those in debt is commendable but is this really the solution that will help the poor financially recover?", "50": "Some people in the US have asserted that forgiving student loan debt is one way to stimulate the economy and give assistance to those in need. One government proposition is to eliminate $10,000 of debt for 'economically distressed' students. Some in US Congress have gone so far as to suggest forgiving up to $50,000 in debt per student borrower, but does forgiving student debt necessarily correlate to helping the economically disadvantaged?\nThe answer is no. This policy is just giving money away to universities and the most affluent students in attendance. Federal Reserve data reveals that the highest-income 40 percent of households owe approximately 60 percent of outstanding student debt, while the lowest 40 percent owe just under 20 percent. This could be due to a combination of factors: students from high-income households are more likely to go to expensive colleges, less likely to receive financial aid, and more likely to have high incomes post-graduation. Plus, the majority of student debt is held by graduate degree earners, who earn approximately 25 percent more than their undergraduate counterparts. Clearly, giving free reign to banks to forgive student debt is a step in the wrong direction.\nOther proposals for broader, long-term student loan plans have some fundamental problems. One idea is to cancel student debt only for undergraduate degrees and for students making less than $125,000.\nThis attempts to address the fact that Congress' previously mentioned student loan forgiveness plan largely helps out the wealthy, but is an adverse incentive for universities to keep raising tuition and for students to choose to major in low-earning degree programs. Colleges have no reason to make their programs more affordable if they believe students will just take out more debt. And, students will feel more comfortable making the irresponsible decision to go tens of thousands of dollars in debt to major in impractical or idealistic subjects if they know their loans will be forgiven.\nThis is especially concerning given the pandemic(大流行病) has rendered a college education practically worthless. Students are paying tens of thousands of dollars per year to live at home and be lectured on the Internet. Do we really want to tell colleges that they can get away with providing below-average service for an outrageous cost?\nIn the case of any of these student debt plans, working-class Americans who chose not to or could not afford to go to college will be subsidizing the education of the professional class. Plumbers and retail workers will be paying for the degrees of doctors and lawyers.\nThe US government's effort to help those in debt is commendable but is this really the solution that will help the poor financially recover?", "51": "If there's one rule that most parents cling to in the confusing, fast-changing world of kids and media, it's \"No screens before age 2.\"\nAs of today, that rule has been thrown out the window.\nThe American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP), which first issued that recommendation back in 1999, has extensively updated and revised its guidelines for children and adolescents to reflect new research and new habits.\nThe new guidelines, especially for very young children, shift the focus from WHAT is on the screen to WHO else is in the room. And in doing so, they raise some intriguing points about the future of learning from media.\nFor babies younger than 18 months, AAP still says no screens at all are the best idea—with one notable exception: Live video chat. Surveys indicate that families already popularly believe that \"Face time doesn't count\", or at least that the benefit of virtual visits with grandparents or other relatives outweighs the potential cost of exposing babies to the laptop or smartphone.\nThe AAP doesn't cite positive evidence that infants actually get something out of this kind of \"conversation\", the way that they clearly do from live social interaction. But there's some observational research that infants as young as six months old are emotionally engaged by playing live peekaboo(躲猫猫) with Grandma online.\nFor infants and toddlers(学步儿童), ages 15 months to 2 years old, there's limited evidence from a couple of very small studies that they can learn new words from educational media, if and only if parents are watching alongside them, repeating what the video says and/or drawing attention to what is on the screen. In other words, treating a video or an app like a picture book is the best bet.\nThe flip side of this is that many studies have actually shown poorer language skills correlated with earlier solo viewing of \"educational\" videos. There's also research that shows language delays in children who watch more TV and start watching earlier. In both cases, the problem seems to be media replacing interaction with people. For this reason, the new AAP guideline has changed from \"avoid all screens under age 2\" to \"avoid solo media use in this age group.\"\nFor preschoolers ages 2 to 5, there's more evidence that they have the ability to transfer knowledge from screens to the real world, including early literacy and math. For this age group, AAP recommends no more than an hour a day of screen use. And, just as with younger children, they want care-givers to take part in screen time: \"Co-view with your children, help children understand what they are seeing, and help them apply what they learn to the world around them.\"", "52": "If there's one rule that most parents cling to in the confusing, fast-changing world of kids and media, it's \"No screens before age 2.\"\nAs of today, that rule has been thrown out the window.\nThe American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP), which first issued that recommendation back in 1999, has extensively updated and revised its guidelines for children and adolescents to reflect new research and new habits.\nThe new guidelines, especially for very young children, shift the focus from WHAT is on the screen to WHO else is in the room. And in doing so, they raise some intriguing points about the future of learning from media.\nFor babies younger than 18 months, AAP still says no screens at all are the best idea—with one notable exception: Live video chat. Surveys indicate that families already popularly believe that \"Face time doesn't count\", or at least that the benefit of virtual visits with grandparents or other relatives outweighs the potential cost of exposing babies to the laptop or smartphone.\nThe AAP doesn't cite positive evidence that infants actually get something out of this kind of \"conversation\", the way that they clearly do from live social interaction. But there's some observational research that infants as young as six months old are emotionally engaged by playing live peekaboo(躲猫猫) with Grandma online.\nFor infants and toddlers(学步儿童), ages 15 months to 2 years old, there's limited evidence from a couple of very small studies that they can learn new words from educational media, if and only if parents are watching alongside them, repeating what the video says and/or drawing attention to what is on the screen. In other words, treating a video or an app like a picture book is the best bet.\nThe flip side of this is that many studies have actually shown poorer language skills correlated with earlier solo viewing of \"educational\" videos. There's also research that shows language delays in children who watch more TV and start watching earlier. In both cases, the problem seems to be media replacing interaction with people. For this reason, the new AAP guideline has changed from \"avoid all screens under age 2\" to \"avoid solo media use in this age group.\"\nFor preschoolers ages 2 to 5, there's more evidence that they have the ability to transfer knowledge from screens to the real world, including early literacy and math. For this age group, AAP recommends no more than an hour a day of screen use. And, just as with younger children, they want care-givers to take part in screen time: \"Co-view with your children, help children understand what they are seeing, and help them apply what they learn to the world around them.\"", "53": "If there's one rule that most parents cling to in the confusing, fast-changing world of kids and media, it's \"No screens before age 2.\"\nAs of today, that rule has been thrown out the window.\nThe American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP), which first issued that recommendation back in 1999, has extensively updated and revised its guidelines for children and adolescents to reflect new research and new habits.\nThe new guidelines, especially for very young children, shift the focus from WHAT is on the screen to WHO else is in the room. And in doing so, they raise some intriguing points about the future of learning from media.\nFor babies younger than 18 months, AAP still says no screens at all are the best idea—with one notable exception: Live video chat. Surveys indicate that families already popularly believe that \"Face time doesn't count\", or at least that the benefit of virtual visits with grandparents or other relatives outweighs the potential cost of exposing babies to the laptop or smartphone.\nThe AAP doesn't cite positive evidence that infants actually get something out of this kind of \"conversation\", the way that they clearly do from live social interaction. But there's some observational research that infants as young as six months old are emotionally engaged by playing live peekaboo(躲猫猫) with Grandma online.\nFor infants and toddlers(学步儿童), ages 15 months to 2 years old, there's limited evidence from a couple of very small studies that they can learn new words from educational media, if and only if parents are watching alongside them, repeating what the video says and/or drawing attention to what is on the screen. In other words, treating a video or an app like a picture book is the best bet.\nThe flip side of this is that many studies have actually shown poorer language skills correlated with earlier solo viewing of \"educational\" videos. There's also research that shows language delays in children who watch more TV and start watching earlier. In both cases, the problem seems to be media replacing interaction with people. For this reason, the new AAP guideline has changed from \"avoid all screens under age 2\" to \"avoid solo media use in this age group.\"\nFor preschoolers ages 2 to 5, there's more evidence that they have the ability to transfer knowledge from screens to the real world, including early literacy and math. For this age group, AAP recommends no more than an hour a day of screen use. And, just as with younger children, they want care-givers to take part in screen time: \"Co-view with your children, help children understand what they are seeing, and help them apply what they learn to the world around them.\"", "54": "If there's one rule that most parents cling to in the confusing, fast-changing world of kids and media, it's \"No screens before age 2.\"\nAs of today, that rule has been thrown out the window.\nThe American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP), which first issued that recommendation back in 1999, has extensively updated and revised its guidelines for children and adolescents to reflect new research and new habits.\nThe new guidelines, especially for very young children, shift the focus from WHAT is on the screen to WHO else is in the room. And in doing so, they raise some intriguing points about the future of learning from media.\nFor babies younger than 18 months, AAP still says no screens at all are the best idea—with one notable exception: Live video chat. Surveys indicate that families already popularly believe that \"Face time doesn't count\", or at least that the benefit of virtual visits with grandparents or other relatives outweighs the potential cost of exposing babies to the laptop or smartphone.\nThe AAP doesn't cite positive evidence that infants actually get something out of this kind of \"conversation\", the way that they clearly do from live social interaction. But there's some observational research that infants as young as six months old are emotionally engaged by playing live peekaboo(躲猫猫) with Grandma online.\nFor infants and toddlers(学步儿童), ages 15 months to 2 years old, there's limited evidence from a couple of very small studies that they can learn new words from educational media, if and only if parents are watching alongside them, repeating what the video says and/or drawing attention to what is on the screen. In other words, treating a video or an app like a picture book is the best bet.\nThe flip side of this is that many studies have actually shown poorer language skills correlated with earlier solo viewing of \"educational\" videos. There's also research that shows language delays in children who watch more TV and start watching earlier. In both cases, the problem seems to be media replacing interaction with people. For this reason, the new AAP guideline has changed from \"avoid all screens under age 2\" to \"avoid solo media use in this age group.\"\nFor preschoolers ages 2 to 5, there's more evidence that they have the ability to transfer knowledge from screens to the real world, including early literacy and math. For this age group, AAP recommends no more than an hour a day of screen use. And, just as with younger children, they want care-givers to take part in screen time: \"Co-view with your children, help children understand what they are seeing, and help them apply what they learn to the world around them.\"", "55": "If there's one rule that most parents cling to in the confusing, fast-changing world of kids and media, it's \"No screens before age 2.\"\nAs of today, that rule has been thrown out the window.\nThe American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP), which first issued that recommendation back in 1999, has extensively updated and revised its guidelines for children and adolescents to reflect new research and new habits.\nThe new guidelines, especially for very young children, shift the focus from WHAT is on the screen to WHO else is in the room. And in doing so, they raise some intriguing points about the future of learning from media.\nFor babies younger than 18 months, AAP still says no screens at all are the best idea—with one notable exception: Live video chat. Surveys indicate that families already popularly believe that \"Face time doesn't count\", or at least that the benefit of virtual visits with grandparents or other relatives outweighs the potential cost of exposing babies to the laptop or smartphone.\nThe AAP doesn't cite positive evidence that infants actually get something out of this kind of \"conversation\", the way that they clearly do from live social interaction. But there's some observational research that infants as young as six months old are emotionally engaged by playing live peekaboo(躲猫猫) with Grandma online.\nFor infants and toddlers(学步儿童), ages 15 months to 2 years old, there's limited evidence from a couple of very small studies that they can learn new words from educational media, if and only if parents are watching alongside them, repeating what the video says and/or drawing attention to what is on the screen. In other words, treating a video or an app like a picture book is the best bet.\nThe flip side of this is that many studies have actually shown poorer language skills correlated with earlier solo viewing of \"educational\" videos. There's also research that shows language delays in children who watch more TV and start watching earlier. In both cases, the problem seems to be media replacing interaction with people. For this reason, the new AAP guideline has changed from \"avoid all screens under age 2\" to \"avoid solo media use in this age group.\"\nFor preschoolers ages 2 to 5, there's more evidence that they have the ability to transfer knowledge from screens to the real world, including early literacy and math. For this age group, AAP recommends no more than an hour a day of screen use. And, just as with younger children, they want care-givers to take part in screen time: \"Co-view with your children, help children understand what they are seeing, and help them apply what they learn to the world around them.\""}, "questions": [{"id": "2022-12-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What message does the woman convey in her book?", "options": ["A) Prioritizing happiness over money.", "B) Joining the club to get her new book.", "C) Managing one's personal finances wisely.", "D) Consuming more only when earning more."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Prioritizing happiness over money.\n【定位】I think that's because of my message, which is making happiness a priority over money.\n我想那是因为我书里的核心讯息——把幸福置于金钱之上。\n【信号】my message is 自报主旨。女方用 that's because of my message, which is making\nhappiness a priority over money 亲口点出 my message（我的核心讯息），这种受访者\n自报观点的句子就是答案信号，无需再推。\n【替换】making happiness a priority over money ↔ Prioritizing happiness over money\n短语换说法、意思全等\n【排除】B) 未提及\n录音里男方说 Welcome to the Book Club 又提 a new book，B 借「book/club」这两个\n原词诱你联想「入会拿书」，但全篇没有任何「加入俱乐部以获取她新书」的内容，是凭原\n词拼出的假选项。\n借用原文：Welcome to the Book Club\nC) 原词陷阱\nManaging one's personal finances wisely 用了书的主题词 personal finances（原文 a\nnew book on personal finances），听着像那么回事，但女方的核心讯息是「幸福优先于\n金钱」这个价值排序，而非笼统的「明智理财」，C 用原词把观点稀释成了空泛口号。\n借用原文：a new book on personal finances\nD) 与原文相反\nConsuming more only when earning more 盗用原文 trying to earn more money, just\nto consume more 的 earn / consume，但那是女方批判的旧循环（结果让人更痛苦），\n不是她要传达的讯息，正话被反着取了。\n借用原文：trying to earn more money, just to consume more, but it made us more miserable", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What do we learn about the woman before she became a writer?", "options": ["A) She was in debt.", "B) She was a financial adviser.", "C) She earned $30,000 a month.", "D) She enjoyed a happy life."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） She was in debt.\n【定位】I earned millions, but by the time I quit my job four years ago to become a writer, I owed\nover $30,000.\n我挣过数百万，但等到四年前我辞职去当作家时，我已欠债超过 3 万美元。\n【信号】转折 but 后才是真处境。句中 I earned millions, but by the time I quit my job…I owed\nover \\$30,000 用 but 转折，前半「挣过数百万」是铺垫，转折后的「欠债三万」才是她当\n作家前的真实处境，转折后即落点。\n【替换】I owed over \\$30,000 ↔ She was in debt\nowe 概括为 in debt\n【排除】B) 偷换概念\nShe was a financial adviser 借书谈 personal finances 的「financial」联想出「理财顾\n问」，但原文说她过去是 working for two decades as an executive（高管），不是理财\n顾问，职业被张冠李戴。\n借用原文：working for two decades as an executive\nC) 原词陷阱\nShe earned \\$30,000 a month 直接搬来原文数字 \\$30,000，但原文 \\$30,000 是 I owed\nover \\$30,000 的「欠债额」，不是月收入；她的收入原文说的是 earned millions。数字\n对、名目全错。\n借用原文：I owed over \\$30,000\nD) 与原文相反\nShe enjoyed a happy life 与原文相反：女方说当年那段循环 made us more miserable\n（更痛苦），过去并不幸福，D 反了极性。\n借用原文：but it made us more miserable", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What does the woman say about one's financial behavior?", "options": ["A) It reflects one's earning power.", "B) It varies with one's environment.", "C) It mirrors one's sense of well-being.", "D) It changes with one's goals in life."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It changes with one's goals in life.\n【定位】Once we make the right things our priority, our goals will change and so will our\nfinancial behavior.\n一旦我们把对的事情当作优先项，我们的目标就会改变，财务行为也随之改变。\n【信号】Once…and so will 的因果链。Once we make the right things our priority, our goals\nwill change and so will our financial behavior 用 Once（一旦）引导条件、so will 承接\n连锁：目标改变带动财务行为改变，财务行为是被「目标」牵着走的，因果链尾即答案。\n【替换】our goals will change and so will our financial behavior ↔ It changes with one's goals in life\nso will 还原成 changes with goals\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nIt reflects one's earning power 蹭了全篇 earn / earned 的高频词，但原文说财务行为由\ngoals 决定，从未说它「反映挣钱能力」，归因对象被换掉了。\n借用原文：trying to earn more money\nB) 原词陷阱\nIt varies with one's environment 的 environment 确实在原文出现过——但那是\nrelationships, the environment or even our hobbies 里「值得珍视的事物」之一，不是\n决定财务行为的因素，词对、关系错。\n借用原文：like relationships, the environment or even our hobbies\nC) 未提及\nIt varies with one's environment 与 B 同样把 environment 当成变量来源，原文从未说\n财务行为随环境变化，纯属拼词造句。\n借用原文：like relationships, the environment or even our hobbies", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does the man say about sharing a home?", "options": ["A) It would give him more time to be with his loved ones.", "B) It would be good for those who value relationships.", "C) It would mean major sacrifices for him.", "D) It would deprive him of his individuality."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （态度观点题） It would mean major sacrifices for him.\n【定位】Those sound like major sacrifices, I could never share my home, I need my own space.\n那些听起来像是重大的牺牲，我绝不可能跟人合住，我需要自己的空间。\n【信号】男方直接应答表态。在女方讲完合住等做法后，男方紧接着应答 Those sound like major\nsacrifices, I could never share my home, I need my own space，这是被问者本人的直接\n表态，态度题的答案就落在这句应答上。\n【替换】Those sound like major sacrifices ↔ It would mean major sacrifices for him\n几乎原词、补出主语 for him\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nIt would give him more time to be with his loved ones 来自女方 giving us an\nopportunity to spend more time with our loved ones，那是女方反驳时说的合住好处，\n不是男方的看法，主体说反了。\n借用原文：giving us an opportunity to spend more time with our loved ones\nB) 张冠李戴\nIt would be good for those who value relationships 对应女方观点 if we value\nrelationships, sharing a home isn't depriving ourselves of space，仍是女方的论调而非\n男方所说，归属错位。\n借用原文：if we value relationships, sharing a home isn't depriving ourselves of space\nD) 原词陷阱\nIt would deprive him of his individuality 蹭了女方原文 sharing a home isn't depriving\nourselves of space 的 depriving，但原文说的是「不会剥夺空间」且是女方所言，D 既盗\n词又把否定说成肯定、还安到男方头上。\n借用原文：sharing a home isn't depriving ourselves of space", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What do we learn about one of the newest additions to the gallery's collection?", "options": ["A) It was the artist's first landscape.", "B) It was a painting by Christopher.", "C) It was donated by the artist herself.", "D) It was displayed at a retirement party."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It was donated by the artist herself.\n【定位】This first piece was a gift to the gallery from the artist herself, and it's quite exquisite.\n这第一幅是艺术家本人赠送给画廊的，相当精美。\n【信号】被问者对新藏品的直接交代。女士问要不要看新藏品后，男士直接介绍 This first piece was\na gift to the gallery from the artist herself——这是被问者对该画来历的正面应答，就是\n答案句。\n【替换】a gift to the gallery from the artist herself ↔ donated by the artist herself\na gift = donated 同义改写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音里女士提到 those the landscapes by Danielle Gregory，男士又说 This first\npiece，选项 A 把 first 与 landscape 拼成『这是她的第一幅风景画』，但 first 只是『第一\n幅展品』之序，原文从未说是画家生涯首作。\n借用原文：This first piece\nB) 张冠李戴\nChristopher 是开头女士称呼的画廊负责人（Thank you for inviting me to the gallery,\nChristopher.），画作者是 Danielle Gregory；选项 B 把这画说成 Christopher 所画，属\n张冠李戴。\n借用原文：Christopher\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音里女士说上次来还是 your predecessor's retirement function（前任的退休活动），\n与这幅画的展示毫无关系；选项 D 把这个退休场合搬来当画作展出地，纯属错配。\n借用原文：your predecessor's retirement function", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the man say about one of the most acclaimed paintings by Danielle Gregory?", "options": ["A) It was the painting that instantly made her rich.", "B) It has cost him a lot of money to purchase it.", "C) It was recently purchased by the gallery.", "D) It is owned by an anonymous collector."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is owned by an anonymous collector.\n【定位】It was purchased at auction by an anonymous collector who lent it to the gallery for display.\nThis composition is one of her most acclaimed paintings.\n它是由一位匿名收藏家在拍卖会上买下的，那人把它借给画廊展出。这幅作品是她最受赞誉\n的画作之一。\n【信号】被问者对『这画怎么来的』的直接应答。女士问 how did you obtain her other piece\nover here?，男士应答 purchased at auction by an anonymous collector who lent it to\nthe gallery——问归属、答归属，就是答案句。\n【替换】purchased at auction by an anonymous collector who lent it to the gallery ↔\nowned by an anonymous collector\n买下并出借 = 仍归其所有\n【排除】A) 未提及\n选项 A 说这画让『她』瞬间致富，录音通篇没提画家因此画暴富；倒是后文说藏家花了 it\nwas substantial 一大笔钱，但那是藏家花钱，不是画家致富，属未提及。\nB) 张冠李戴\n录音确实说买这画花了一大笔（cost that collector a small fortune、it was\nsubstantial），但花钱的是匿名藏家，不是男士本人；选项 B 用 cost him 把买家偷换成男\n士。\n借用原文：It must have cost that collector a small fortune\nC) 与原文相反\n录音原句确有 purchased at auction，但买家是匿名藏家，不是画廊；选项 C 把『画廊近\n期购入』套上 purchased 一词，恰与原文相反（画廊只是借展、并未买）。\n借用原文：purchased at auction by an anonymous collector", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "Why does the woman say she can feel immersed in the scene in the painting?", "options": ["A) It reflects her emotions.", "B) It contains ample details.", "C) It appears perfectly symmetrical.", "D) It depicts the beauty of desolation."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It contains ample details.\n【定位】There's so much detail in this painting. I feel like I can really immerse myself in the scene.\n这幅画里有这么多细节。我感觉自己真能沉浸到画面之中。\n【信号】感受句前的原因陈述。女士先说 There's so much detail in this painting，紧接着才说 I\ncan really immerse myself in the scene——『大量细节』是她能沉浸的原因，先因后\n果，前句即落点。\n【替换】There's so much detail in this painting ↔ contains ample details\nso much detail = ample details\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音里只有 You can sense the desolation 这类气氛话，且女士谈沉浸时给的理由是细节\n多，不是『画反映她的情感』；选项 A 的 emotions 录音并未作为沉浸之由提出，属未提\n及。\nC) 答非所问\n女士确实说了 I particularly like the symmetry created by the reflection，但那是她另外\n补充喜欢的一点，不是『能沉浸』的原因；本题问的沉浸之由是前一句的 so much detail，\n选项 C 把相邻却不同句的对称搬来顶替。\n借用原文：the symmetry created by the reflection of the mountain in the lake\nD) 原词陷阱\n荒凉之美 You can sense the desolation in the picture 是男士谈另一幅戈壁画作时的话，\n与女士此处『因细节多而沉浸』无关；选项 D 把 desolation 搬来当沉浸理由，属答非所\n问。\n借用原文：You can sense the desolation in the picture", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "How does the man describe Danielle Gregory?", "options": ["A) She is eccentric like any other artist.", "B) She is a very nice and intelligent artist.", "C) She is as lucky as any acclaimed artist.", "D) She is one of the most productive artists."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度观点题） She is a very nice and intelligent artist.\n【定位】Quite the opposite. In fact, she's not at all eccentric. I would say she's one of the most\neasygoing and intelligent people I know.\n恰恰相反。事实上，她一点也不古怪。我会说她是我认识的最随和、最聪明的人之一。\n【信号】转折否定后才是真评价。女士猜她古怪，男士先 Quite the opposite、再 not at all\neccentric 把猜测顶回去，转折否定之后的 easygoing and intelligent 才是他的真实评价，\n正是答案句。\n【替换】she's one of the most easygoing and intelligent people I know ↔\nShe is a very nice and intelligent artist.\neasygoing = nice，intelligent 原义保留\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\neccentric 录音里确实出现，但男士是 she's not at all eccentric 加以否定的；选项 A 取了\n被否定掉的那个词，与男士本意正好相反。\n借用原文：she's not at all eccentric\nC) 张冠李戴\n录音里 I was lucky enough to attend the award ceremony 说的是男士自己幸运，不是\n画家幸运；选项 C 把 lucky 挪到对画家的评价上，属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：I was lucky enough to attend the award ceremony\nD) 未提及\n录音说她最受赞誉、随和聪明，但从未说她是最高产的画家；选项 D 的 most productive\n录音里没有对应，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What does the passage say about forgiveness?", "options": ["A) It is vital to one's mental health.", "B) It leads to reconciliation and peace.", "C) It promotes interpersonal relationship.", "D) It keeps one from traumatic experience."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It is vital to one's mental health.\n【定位】Forgiveness is vitally important for the mental health of certain victims.\n对某些受害者而言，宽恕对其心理健康至关重要。\n【信号】正面界定句 is vitally important for。录音里 Forgiveness is vitally important for the\nmental health of certain victims. 用'X is important for…'的正面陈述直接给宽恕定价值，\n是典型的'下定义/给判断'信号句，细节定义题的答案就落在这种句子上。\n【替换】vitally important for the mental health ↔ vital to one's mental health\n副词转形容词、介词换说法\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nIt leads to reconciliation and peace. 看似命中 reconciliation，但录音 Forgiveness\ndoesn't mean reconciliation. 恰恰说宽恕'不等于'和解，选项把被否定的词当成正面结论，\n方向相反。\n借用原文：Forgiveness doesn't mean reconciliation.\nC) 未提及\nIt promotes interpersonal relationship. 与原文相反：录音 We don't have to return to\nthe same relationship 说宽恕后不必回到原来的关系，根本没说宽恕能'促进'人际关系，\npromotes 是无中生有又恰好反向。\n借用原文：We don't have to return to the same relationship\nD) 原词陷阱\nIt keeps one from traumatic experience. 借了录音里的 trauma 一词，但原文 keeping\nthem emotionally engaged in an injustice or trauma 说的是'宽恕让人不再深陷于创伤\n情绪'，不是'让人远离/避免创伤经历(keeps one from traumatic experience)'，偷换了\ntrauma 的语境。\n借用原文：keeping them emotionally engaged in an injustice or trauma", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "When is forgiveness especially challenging?", "options": ["A) When the offender has power over the victim.", "B) When the offender is not willing to apologize.", "C) When the offender is not duly penalized.", "D) When the offender adds insult to injury."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） When the offender is not willing to apologize.\n【定位】This is especially true when the offending party offers an insincere apology, or maybe they\nhaven't offered anything at all.\n尤其当冒犯方道歉并不真诚，或者干脆什么都没表示时，更是如此。\n【信号】especially true when 引出难点条件。录音 This is especially true when the offending\nparty offers an insincere apology, or maybe they haven't offered anything at all. 紧接\n在 'Forgiveness can be challenging.' 之后，用 especially true when 把'最难'的条件点\n名，正是题干 especially challenging 的答案信号句。\n【替换】they haven't offered anything at all ↔ not willing to apologize\n'压根没道歉'=不愿道歉\noffers an insincere apology ↔ not willing to apologize\n'道歉不真诚'≈无诚意道歉\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nWhen the offender has power over the victim. 里的'权力'像呼应录音 the victim\nbecomes more empowered，但原文 empowered 说的是'受害者更有力量(选择不宽\n恕)'，恰恰是 victim 被赋权，而非 offender 对 victim 有权力，主体被掉包。\n借用原文：the victim becomes more empowered when they give themselves permission not\nto forgive\nC) 未提及\nWhen the offender is not duly penalized. 讲'冒犯者没被适当惩罚'，录音通篇谈道歉是否\n真诚、动机是否恶意，从没提'惩罚(penalize)'这一维度，属凭常识脑补的未提及项。\nD) 过度推断\nWhen the offender adds insult to injury.('火上浇油/雪上加霜')听起来也像'让宽恕更难'的\n情形，但录音点名的难点只有'道歉不真诚或不道歉'，并未提冒犯者'再次加害'，属过度推\n断。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What should one do in order to forgive the offender?", "options": ["A) Talk with the offender calmly.", "B) Accept the offender's apology.", "C) Find out why he committed the offense.", "D) Determine how serious the offense was."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Find out why he committed the offense.\n【定位】It's important to cultivate forgiveness by developing compassion for the offender, reflect on\nwhether the act was due to malicious intent, or whether it was caused by challenging\ncircumstances in the offender's life.\n重要的是要通过对冒犯者培养同情心来培育宽恕，去反思那件事究竟是出于恶意，还是由冒\n犯者生活中的艰难处境所致。\n【信号】It's important to…by… 引出方法。录音 It's important to cultivate forgiveness by\ndeveloping compassion for the offender, reflect on whether the act was due to\nmalicious intent, or whether it was caused by challenging circumstances 用 'It's\nimportant to…by + 做法' 的建议句式直接给出培养宽恕的方法，是方法题的答案信号句。\n【替换】reflect on whether the act was due to malicious intent, or whether it was caused by challenging\ncircumstances in the offender's life\n↔ Find out why he committed the offense\n'反思动机是恶意还是处境'=弄清原因\n【排除】A) 未提及\nTalk with the offender calmly. 听上去是化解冲突的好办法，但录音讲的方法是'对冒犯者\n培养同情、反思其动机'，从没提'与对方平静交谈(talk with…calmly)'，属合理却未提及的\n脑补做法。\nB) 原词陷阱\nAccept the offender's apology. 借了录音里的 apology 一词，但原文 the offending\nparty offers an insincere apology, or maybe they haven't offered anything at all 说的\n是道歉往往不真诚甚至没有道歉，宽恕并不以'接受道歉'为前提，选项与文意相悖。\n借用原文：This is especially true when the offending party offers an insincere apology, or maybe\nthey haven't offered anything at all.\nD) 原词陷阱\nDetermine how serious the offense was. 借了录音里反复出现的 offense，但原文要人反\n思的是冒犯的'原因(why/intent)'，不是冒犯的'严重程度(how serious)'，偷换了反思的对\n象。\n借用原文：reflect on whether the act was due to malicious intent, or whether it was caused by\nchallenging circumstances in the offender's life", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What does the passage say about the Glasgow subway in the 1960s?", "options": ["A) The number of passengers dropped sharply.", "B) It served more and more commuters.", "C) The number of stations increased to 50.", "D) It became the longest in the United Kingdom."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The number of passengers dropped sharply.\n【定位】In the 1960s, there was a decline in the shipbuilding industry and the popularity of private\ntransport grew. As a result, the subway saw a rapid decline in ridership.\n在 20 世纪 60 年代，造船业衰退、私人交通工具日益普及。结果，地铁的客流量急剧\n下降。\n【信号】时间词 + 因果信号 As a result。听到 In the 1960s 锁定时间，再抓住因果信号 As a\nresult，结果句 the subway saw a rapid decline in ridership 才是承载答案的落点——这\n是篇章题靠因果结论句定位的典型。\n【替换】a rapid decline in ridership ↔ The number of passengers dropped sharply\n同义改写：ridership=客流人数，rapid decline=dropped sharply\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nIt served more and more commuters. 把方向反了——原文 1960 年代是 a rapid\ndecline in ridership（客流骤降），B 却说服务越来越多通勤者，正好相反。\"客流增多\" 是\n录音里别的时期（早年高峰）的情形，被挪到 1960 年代头上。\n借用原文：a rapid decline in ridership\nC) 未提及\nThe number of stations increased to 50. 凭空捏造。原文明说 it has never been\nexpanded, remaining as a single loop line with a mere 15 stations，站数一直是 15 且\n从未扩建，根本没有增到 50 一说。\nD) 原词陷阱\nIt became the longest in the United Kingdom. 借 \"最长\" 这类醒目说法制造诱惑，但原\n文谈的是它是 the world's third underground railway（世界第三条地下铁），讲的是 \"第\n三早\" 而非 \"全英最长\"，把 \"第三\" 偷换成 \"最长\"、把 \"世界范围的早\" 偷换成 \"英国范围的\n长\"。\n借用原文：the world's third underground railway, after London and Budapest", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "Why was the Glasgow subway closed in 1977?", "options": ["A) To increase capacity to meet growing needs.", "B) To make way for other means of transport.", "C) To have its systems modernised.", "D) To avoid further financial losses."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） To have its systems modernised.\n【定位】It ran with little further change until 1977 when its new operators closed it for major\nmodernisation investment.\n它一直没怎么变，直到 1977 年新运营方将其关闭，以进行大规模的现代化改造投资。\n【信号】目的信号 for + 时间词 1977。听到 until 1977 锁定时间后，紧跟的介词 for major\nmodernisation investment 里的 for 正是表目的的信号词，for 后面的成分就是关闭的\n原因。\n【替换】for major modernisation investment ↔ To have its systems modernised\n同根词改写：modernisation→modernised，名词转动词被动\n【排除】A) 未提及\nTo increase capacity to meet growing needs. 录音没有依据。原文反而强调它 has never\nbeen expanded（从未扩建），与 \"增加运力\" 相悖，更没说是为应对需求增长而关闭。\nB) 未提及\nTo make way for other means of transport. 是无中生有。原文后文只说改造后地铁成了\na viable alternative to other forms of transport（其他交通的替代选择），是 \"地铁替代\n别的交通\"，绝非 \"为别的交通让路\"，B 把关系搞反且与 1977 关闭目的无关。\n借用原文：a viable alternative to other forms of transport\nD) 原词陷阱\nTo avoid further financial losses. 听感上呼应了前文客流下降，像是 \"亏损\"，但原文从未\n把关闭归因于避免财务亏损，1977 关闭明确是 for major modernisation investment。它\n借第一段衰退的语境制造 \"亏损\" 联想，属过度推断式的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：a rapid decline in ridership", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "Why does the Glasgow subway remain important today?", "options": ["A) It is generally recognised as a world heritage site.", "B) It is the fastest way to reach the city's south side.", "C) It constitutes a source of revenue for the city.", "D) It helps reduce traffic jams in the city centre."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It helps reduce traffic jams in the city centre.\n【定位】It serves as a viable alternative to other forms of transport and has gone a long way to\nalleviate traffic jams in the city centre.\n它成为其他交通方式的一个可行替代选择，并在很大程度上缓解了市中心的交通拥堵。\n【信号】作用信号 serves as / alleviate。听到 It serves as a viable alternative 与 alleviate\ntraffic jams in the city centre 这类讲贡献作用的动词，就是承载 \"为何重要\" 的答案句。\n【替换】alleviate traffic jams in the city centre ↔ helps reduce traffic jams in the city centre\n近义词替换：alleviate=help reduce\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nIt is generally recognised as a world heritage site. 盗用了原文 It is generally\nrecognised as the world's third underground railway 的 \"generally recognised as\" 整\n个句式，但原文认的是 \"世界第三条地下铁\"，不是 \"世界遗产\"，把 third underground\nrailway 偷换成 world heritage site。这也答非所问：该句讲历史地位，并非它 today 重要\n的原因。\n借用原文：It is generally recognised as the world's third underground railway\nB) 原词陷阱\nIt is the fastest way to reach the city's south side. 借了原文 the shipyard workers on\nthe south side of the city 的 south side 字眼，但原文只说早年它服务南区造船工人，从\n未说它是去南区 \"最快\" 的方式，fastest 是凭空添加的最高级。\n借用原文：the shipyard workers on the south side of the city\nC) 未提及\nIt constitutes a source of revenue for the city. 录音通篇没有提到地铁为城市创收/带来财\n政收入，是无中生有的功能。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the passage say about all subway stations in Glasgow nowadays?", "options": ["A) They are usually crowded.", "B) They use high-tech systems.", "C) They accept smartcards only.", "D) They are colourfully decorated."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They use high-tech systems.\n【定位】Recently, high-tech systems such as smart card ticketing machines and smart gates are used\nacross all subway stations.\n近来，诸如智能卡售票机和智能闸机之类的高科技系统已在所有地铁站投入使用。\n【信号】现状信号 Recently + 范围词 all stations。听到 Recently 即进入讲现状的部分，high-\ntech systems such as smart card ticketing machines and smart gates are used across\nall subway stations 里 all subway stations 与题干 all stations 对应，正是答案句。\n【替换】high-tech systems such as smart card ticketing machines and smart gates ↔\nThey use high-tech systems\n概括替换：列举的具体例子→上位概括 high-tech systems\n【排除】A) 未提及\nThey are usually crowded. 录音从未提到车站拥挤，是无中生有。原文末段讲的是高科技\n系统，与拥挤无关。\nC) 原词陷阱\nThey accept smartcards only. 盗用原文 smart card ticketing machines 的 smart\ncard，但原文只说智能卡是高科技系统之一、很方便，从未说车站 \"只收\" 智能卡。only 是\n凭空加的绝对范围词，把 \"用了智能卡\" 偷改成 \"只收智能卡\"。\n借用原文：smart card ticketing machines and smart gates\nD) 张冠李戴\nThey are colourfully decorated. 借了原文 the colour scheme of the train carriages\nwas updated 的 colour 字眼，但那是为纪念百年更新了\"车厢\"的配色，对象是 train\ncarriages，且属 1996 年的旧事，并非如今 \"所有车站\" 装饰艳丽，对象和时间都被张冠李\n戴。\n借用原文：the colour scheme of the train carriages was updated", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What does the speaker say about the bears in national parks now?", "options": ["A) They are quite friendly to humans.", "B) They are shrinking in numbers.", "C) They are unafraid of humans.", "D) They are especially fond of garbage."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They are unafraid of humans.\n【定位】Since national parks are wildlife sanctuaries where no disturbance of the native animals is\nallowed, years of protection have served to break down the wild bears' fear of humans.\n由于国家公园是禁止任何打扰本地动物的野生动物保护区，多年的保护已经逐渐瓦解了野生\n熊对人类的恐惧。\n【信号】因果句的结果落点。years of protection have served to break down the wild bears'\nfear of humans 是\"多年保护\"导致的结果，\"瓦解了对人的恐惧\"正是对熊现状的概括，这\n种\"原因—结果\"句的结果端就是答案落点。\n【替换】break down the wild bears' fear of humans ↔ They are unafraid of humans.\n\"瓦解恐惧\"=\"不再害怕\"\n【排除】A) 过度推断\n原文只说熊 break down the wild bears' fear of humans（不再怕人），但\"不怕\"不等\n于\"友好\"——后文讲熊咬人抓人，恰恰说明它们并不友好。They are quite friendly to\nhumans. 是把\"不怕\"硬升级成\"友好\"，推过了头。\n借用原文：break down the wild bears' fear of humans\nB) 与原文相反\n录音里只在第一段说 the population of bears runs into the hundreds（熊有成百上\n千），根本没说数量在减少。They are shrinking in numbers. 与原文给出的\"数量众多\"印\n象相反，属与原文相反。\n借用原文：the population of bears runs into the hundreds\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念到 garbage is quite acceptable（垃圾它们也吃），They are especially fond\nof garbage. 就盗用了 garbage 这个原词。但原文是说熊\"几乎什么都吃，垃圾也接受\"，并\n非\"特别偏爱垃圾\"，更不是对熊\"现状\"的总概括，是答非所问的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：garbage is quite acceptable", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What does the speaker say about visitors feeding bears in national parks?", "options": ["A) It is strictly forbidden.", "B) It is an uncommon sight.", "C) It is a gesture of human generosity.", "D) It is allowed only in certain areas."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It is strictly forbidden.\n【定位】An occasional offer of food from a park visitor, an illegal and dangerous practice, makes\nbeggars of them.\n公园游客偶尔给熊喂食，这是一种违法又危险的行为，会把熊变成乞食者。\n【信号】插入语直接定性 illegal。an illegal and dangerous practice 是夹在句中的插入语，直接\n给\"游客喂食\"贴上\"违法且危险\"的定性标签，这种紧贴行为的评判性插入语就是答案信号；\n第三段 Park rules prohibit the feeding of bears. 再次印证。\n【替换】an illegal and dangerous practice ↔ It is strictly forbidden.\nillegal/prohibit = strictly forbidden\n【排除】B) 答非所问\n原文说游客喂食只是 An occasional offer of food（偶尔给食），但整段都在讲熊巡逻、讨\n食、被喂出问题，可见这事并不罕见；题问的也是\"喂熊\"的性质（违法），而非它\"常不常\n见\"。It is an uncommon sight. 答非所问。\n借用原文：An occasional offer of food from a park visitor\nC) 与原文相反\n录音第三段确实出现 misguided kindness（误导的善意），并提到熊指望\"这种慷慨\"，\ngenerosity 一词录音真念过，于是 It is a gesture of human generosity. 把它正面化。但\n作者明说这是\"misguided（错误的）\"善意，是正话反说，并非赞许喂熊是慷慨之举。\n借用原文：misguided kindness\nD) 与原文相反\n原文是 Park rules prohibit the feeding of bears.（规定禁止喂熊），全面禁止、没有任\n何\"某些区域可以喂\"的说法。It is allowed only in certain areas. 凭空造出\"分区允许\"，与\n原文相反。\n借用原文：Park rules prohibit the feeding of bears.", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What is the speaker's advice to people who carry some food while hiking on a park trail?", "options": ["A) Share their food with the bear they see.", "B) Be prepared to run into a hungry bear.", "C) Try to be friendly with the bear they meet.", "D) Refrain from teasing bears with cubs."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Be prepared to run into a hungry bear.\n【定位】Because bears prefer roads and campgrounds, the possibility of a hiker meeting up with a\nbear along park trails is small, but there's always that chance, for a bear seems to know if you\nare carrying a lunch or a candy bar. He may even insist on taking it.\n由于熊更喜欢马路和营地，徒步者在公园小径上遇到熊的可能性很小，但总有那么一点几\n率，因为熊似乎能察觉你是否带着午餐或糖果。它甚至可能非要把它抢走。\n【信号】转折 but 之后才是真意。前半 the possibility of a hiker meeting up with a bear along\npark trails is small 是铺垫，but there's always that chance 的 but 一转，才点出\"仍有可\n能遇上、且熊会盯上你的食物甚至抢走\"——转折后这层提醒就是 advice 的落点。\n【替换】there's always that chance ↔ Be prepared to run into a hungry bear.\n\"总有机会遇上\"=\"要有遇熊的准备\"\nHe may even insist on taking it ↔ a hungry bear\n\"非要抢食\"印证\"饿熊\"\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nShare their food with the bear they see. 是\"主动喂熊\"，但全文反复说喂熊违法危险（an\nillegal and dangerous practice、Park rules prohibit the feeding of bears.），作者绝不\n会建议分食物给熊，与原文立场相反。\n借用原文：Park rules prohibit the feeding of bears.\nC) 与原文相反\nTry to be friendly with the bear they meet. 让人主动亲近熊，但录音通篇在讲熊咬人抓\n人、会抢食，提醒的是\"提防\"而非\"友好\"。这是把第 16 题埋的 friendly 一词挪过来制造的\n诱惑，与作者本意相反。\n借用原文：He may even insist on taking it\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音第四段确有带幼崽的母熊伤人的例子（the mother bear appeared），cubs 的画面录\n音真出现过，Refrain from teasing bears with cubs. 就借此设套。但那只是\"事故举例\"，\n作者末段给徒步者的建议并不是\"别逗带崽的熊\"，属答非所问的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：One man was in the process of feeding two small cubs", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does the speaker say about prejudice?", "options": ["A) It refers to opinions that are radical and widespread.", "B) It means making judgments without adequate knowledge.", "C) It refers to deep-rooted beliefs about someone or something.", "D) It means sticking to one's judgments even when proved wrong."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It means making judgments without adequate knowledge.\n【定位】Forming opinions about someone or something before really knowing them well is called\nprejudice. \"Pre-\" means before, and \"judice\" refers to judgment. Hence, prejudice means to\njudge before having adequate knowledge.\n在真正了解某人或某物之前就对其形成看法，这叫作偏见。\"Pre-\"意为\"之\n前\"，\"judice\"指\"判断\"。因此，偏见就是在没有足够认识之前就下判断。\n【信号】定义标志is called与Hence总结。is called prejudice 给出概念名称、紧接 Hence,\nprejudice means to judge before having adequate knowledge 用 Hence 收束成一句\n完整定义，这个\"下定义+总结\"信号让它成为答案句。\n【替换】to judge before having adequate knowledge ↔ making judgments without adequate knowledge\njudge=making judgments且before=without\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nopinions that are radical and widespread 借了录音里的 opinions 一词，但原文从未说\n偏见是\"激进而广泛的（radical and widespread）\"观点，激进/广泛是凭空加的限定，属听\n到 opinions 就想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：hearing something good or bad about a person, a place or thing can influence\nour opinions\nC) 偷换概念\ndeep-rooted beliefs about someone or something 蹭了原文反复出现的 about\nsomeone or something，但把偏见说成\"根深蒂固的信念\"，而原文定义强调的是\"了解/认\n识之前\"就下判断，时间点完全不同，属偷换概念。\n借用原文：Forming opinions about someone or something before really knowing them well\nD) 答非所问\nsticking to one's judgments even when proved wrong 用了 judgments 一词，但说的\n是\"被证明错了仍坚持\"，是事后固执；原文定义说的是\"了解之前\"就下判断，是事前，与原\n文相反/错位。\n借用原文：prejudice means to judge before having adequate knowledge", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "Why does the speaker say prejudiced attitudes are dangerous?", "options": ["A) They often lead to war between religious groups.", "B) They keep certain occupations from thriving.", "C) They allow myths and half-truths to persist.", "D) They prevent us from getting to the truth."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They prevent us from getting to the truth.\n【定位】Prejudiced attitudes are usually based on myths, half-truths or incorrect information, and they\nare dangerous because they can keep us from learning the truth about someone or something.\n偏见态度通常建立在谬误、半真半假的说法或错误信息之上，它们之所以危险，是因为它们\n会妨碍我们了解关于某人或某物的真相。\n【信号】dangerous because因果信号。they are dangerous because they can keep us from\nlearning the truth 里 dangerous 后跟 because，因果信号直接把\"危险的原因\"端出来，\nbecause 之后那半句就是答案句。\n【替换】keep us from learning the truth ↔ prevent us from getting to the truth\nkeep from=prevent from且learning=getting to\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nwar between religious groups 借了原文 religion/后文 religious 的影子，但原文此处说\n危害是\"妨碍了解真相\"，并未说偏见\"导致宗教群体间的战争\"；战争是过度引申，原文只说\n极端偏见 caused deaths，没说\"宗教群体间开战\"。\n借用原文：differences in their race, religion, gender, or occupation\nB) 张冠李戴\nkeep certain occupations from thriving 蹭了原文 occupation 一词，但原文说偏见 keep\npeople apart（让人彼此疏远），第三段说妨碍人 getting a job or a promotion（找工\n作/晋升），从没说让\"某些职业无法繁荣\"，属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：from getting a job or a promotion\nC) 原词陷阱\nallow myths and half-truths to persist 直接盗用原文 myths, half-truths，极具迷惑性；\n但原文说偏见是\"基于（based on）myths/half-truths\"，谬误是偏见的成因/来源，不是\nbecause 后给出的\"危险原因\"，答非所问。\n借用原文：Prejudiced attitudes are usually based on myths, half-truths or incorrect information", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "When does the speaker say we begin to develop prejudiced attitudes?", "options": ["A) When we start to feel superior.", "B) When we mix with prejudiced people.", "C) When we live in an isolated neighborhood.", "D) When we try to keep up with those around us."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） When we start to feel superior.\n【定位】If feelings of pride begin to turn to feelings of superiority when we think that our group or our\nbeliefs are better than those around us, however, then we begin to develop prejudiced\nattitudes that can be harmful.\n如果自豪感开始转变为优越感——当我们认为自己的群体或信念比周围人更好时——然而，\n那时我们就开始发展出可能有害的偏见态度。\n【信号】If…turn to superiority条件信号。If feelings of pride begin to turn to feelings of\nsuperiority 用 If 引出条件、then we begin to develop prejudiced attitudes 给出结果，\n这个\"条件→产生偏见\"的因果/条件信号让它成为答案句。\n【替换】feelings of pride begin to turn to feelings of superiority ↔ When we start to feel superior\nturn to feelings of superiority=start to feel superior\n【排除】B) 未提及\nWhen we mix with prejudiced people（与有偏见的人来往时）听上去合理，但原文给的\n触发条件是\"自豪转为优越感\"，从没说\"和有偏见的人相处\"会让我们产生偏见，属未提及。\nC) 原词陷阱\nlive in an isolated neighborhood 盗用了原文 living in any neighborhood 的\nneighborhood 一词；但原文里 neighborhood 出现在\"偏见使某群体无法随意居住\"的危\n害举例中，是偏见的后果，不是产生偏见的条件，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：from living in any neighborhood they want\nD) 与原文相反\nWhen we try to keep up with those around us（努力跟上周围人）与原文 better than\nthose around us（觉得自己比周围人更好）方向相反：原文是\"自觉高人一等\"，选项却\n是\"努力追赶别人\"，与原文相反。\n借用原文：our group or our beliefs are better than those around us", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "How did the speaker feel when he started high school?", "options": ["A) Motivated.", "B) Disappointed.", "C) Perplexed.", "D) Shocked."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） Shocked.\n【定位】When I started high school, it was a shock.\n我上高中的时候，那真是一记冲击。\n【信号】直陈感受句 it was a shock。开篇第一句 it was a shock 就用 it was + 情绪名词直接定调\n入学感受，这种讲者自陈心情的句子就是态度题的答案句。\n【替换】it was a shock ↔ Shocked.\n名词 shock 转形容词 Shocked\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nMotivated. 是被激励的积极情绪，而原文 it was a shock 是负面冲击，方向相反。\n借用原文：it was a shock\nB) 偷换概念\nDisappointed. 是失望，原文是 shock（震惊冲击），二者都偏负面但情绪类型不同，原文\n没有“期望落空”的意思。\nC) 偷换概念\nPerplexed. 是困惑、想不明白，原文 shock 强调的是突然的强烈冲击，并非智力上的困\n惑。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What did the speaker once think of teenage friendships?", "options": ["A) They would change with the passage of time.", "B) They would benefit young people's adult life.", "C) They would help kids grow.", "D) They would last a lifetime."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They would last a lifetime.\n【定位】During those teenage years, I built friendships that I thought would last a lifetime.\n在那些青少年岁月里，我建立了一些我以为会维系一辈子的友谊。\n【信号】I thought 引出当初的判断。that I thought would last a lifetime 里的 I thought（我当\n初以为）正对题干的 once think，是讲者亲口给出的当年看法，定为答案句。\n【替换】would last a lifetime ↔ They would last a lifetime.\n几乎原句照搬\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nThey would change with the passage of time. 说友谊会随时间改变，这恰恰是讲者后来\n才发现的现实（people drift apart），不是他当初 thought 的看法，张冠李戴了时间点。\n借用原文：people drift apart\nB) 未提及\nThey would benefit young people's adult life. 谈友谊对成年生活的好处，录音只说他以\n为友谊会持久，从未谈“对成年生活有益”，属无中生有。\nC) 未提及\nThey would help kids grow. 说友谊帮孩子成长，录音里讲者对青少年友谊的判断只有“会\n持续一辈子”，没有“助成长”这一层。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What do we learn about the speaker when he returned from the Vietnam War?", "options": ["A) He had become mature.", "B) He suffered poor health.", "C) He had lots of stories to tell.", "D) He regretted leaving Vietnam."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （推断题） He had become mature.\n【定位】I couldn't relate to the friends I had had in high school. They still seemed childish, concerned\nwith childish things that weren't important to me.\n我没法再和高中时的那些朋友产生共鸣了。他们在我看来还是很幼稚，只在意一些对我而言\n已不重要的幼稚事情。\n【信号】以朋友的“幼稚”反衬自己的成熟。讲者说朋友 still seemed childish、还在关心 childish\nthings that weren't important to me，用对方的幼稚反衬出自己已经变了——经历战争后\n心智超前于同龄人，这处对比就是归纳点。\n【替换】They still seemed childish, concerned with childish things that weren't important to me ↔\nHe had become mature.\n“别人幼稚”反向等于“我成熟”\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n讲者说自己 burned out, and tired，He suffered poor health. 盗用这种“累、垮”的字眼当\n身体得病，但 burned out 指心力交瘁，原文并未说他身体患病。\n借用原文：burned out, and tired\nC) 过度推断\nHe had lots of stories to tell. 看似贴合“经历越战、活了十辈子”，但原文 as though I had\nlived 10 lifetimes 是形容沧桑成熟，不是说他爱讲故事、有谈资，答非所问。\n借用原文：as though I had lived 10 lifetimes\nD) 未提及\nHe regretted leaving Vietnam. 说他后悔离开越南，原文只讲他对在越南的所见所为感到\n痛苦，从没说后悔离开那里，属无中生有且方向也不对。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What does the speaker try to do as a teacher?", "options": ["A) Make friends with his students.", "B) Show his students how to do their best.", "C) Help his students get through the growing pains.", "D) Share his personal experience with his students."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （目的题） Help his students get through the growing pains.\n【定位】I look at my students and see them struggling with many of the very things I struggled with\nmany years ago. As a teacher, I try to help them over the rough spots as best I can.\n我看着我的学生，看到他们正为许多当年我也挣扎过的同样的事而苦苦挣扎。作为一名老\n师，我尽我所能帮他们渡过那些艰难的关口。\n【信号】As a teacher, I try to… 的收束主句。结尾 As a teacher, I try to help them over the\nrough spots 用 I try to help… 直陈自己当老师的做法，是全篇落点句，正对题干 try\nto do。\n【替换】help them over the rough spots ↔ Help his students get through the growing pains.\nrough spots = growing pains\nstruggling with many of the very things I struggled with ↔\nHelp his students get through the growing pains.\n学生的挣扎即成长之痛\n【排除】A) 未提及\nMake friends with his students. 说和学生交朋友，结尾只说 I try to help them over the\nrough spots（帮他们渡难关），从没说要与学生做朋友，无中生有。\nB) 偷换概念\nShow his students how to do their best. 说教学生如何做到最好，原文是 help…over\nthe rough spots（帮他们渡过难关），偏向陪伴扶持，不是示范如何取得最好表现，偷换\n了做法。\nD) 原词陷阱\nShare his personal experience with his students. 借前文大段个人经历制造诱惑，但结尾\n的做法是 help them over the rough spots，是“帮”不是“讲给学生听”，他回顾经历只为解\n释自己懂学生的处境，并未说要把经历分享给学生，答非所问。\n借用原文：I look at my students and see them struggling with many of the very things I struggled\nwith many years ago", "points": ["听力·讲座", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetic", "B) appropriate", "C) clusters", "D) competitive", "E) exploiting", "F) fiscal", "G) instances", "H) rehabilitated", "I) reproduced", "J) revenues", "K) safeguarding", "L) ship", "M) strategic", "N) transcend", "O) undergoing"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) appropriate\n形容词\n【词性槽】wherever + __ 让步从句省略\n【依据】wherever appropriate 是英文公文/学术死搭配\nwherever appropriate（在合适的地方/凡合适之处）是英文公文与学术写作的经典短语，\n与上文\"把那些能力移植到合适的地方\"的全球战略逻辑完美匹配。\n【竞争词】A) aesthetic 话题错\nwherever aesthetic 在语义上无意义；本句讲战略选址，不是审美判断。\nF) fiscal 话题错\nwherever fiscal 不构成英文表达；fiscal 修饰 year/policy 等具体名词，不用作\"地点合适与\n否\"的判断。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetic", "B) appropriate", "C) clusters", "D) competitive", "E) exploiting", "F) fiscal", "G) instances", "H) rehabilitated", "I) reproduced", "J) revenues", "K) safeguarding", "L) ship", "M) strategic", "N) transcend", "O) undergoing"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) competitive\n形容词\n【词性槽】名词 advantage 前的定语位\n【依据】competitive advantage 是商科死搭配\n全文在讨论企业如何打造全球优势（to build a platform of capabilities ... ahead of the\ncompetition），competitive advantage（竞争优势）是商业战略文献中的高频固定搭\n配，与上文 ahead of the competition 形成完美呼应。\n【竞争词】A) aesthetic 义域偏窄、与 advantage 不搭\naesthetic advantage（美学优势）不是固定搭配，且本句讲的是 Apple 在全球的整体优势，不\n是单指美学；A 留给下文真正讨论 design 的空。\nB) appropriate appropriate advantage 不成立\nappropriate 修饰 advantage 在语义上别扭——\"恰当的优势\"无意义；它的典型搭配是\nwherever appropriate / appropriate measures。\nF) fiscal 话题不对\nfiscal 指财政/财务的，全文没有讨论税收或财政政策，与本句\"独特能力带来的优势\"语义无关。\nM) strategic 义近但被下文锁定\nstrategic advantage 虽勉强通，但 M 在第 34 空有更刚性的固定搭配 strategic and\noperational finesse；按\"先难后易\"原则，本空让位、把 M 留给 34。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetic", "B) appropriate", "C) clusters", "D) competitive", "E) exploiting", "F) fiscal", "G) instances", "H) rehabilitated", "I) reproduced", "J) revenues", "K) safeguarding", "L) ship", "M) strategic", "N) transcend", "O) undergoing"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) aesthetic\n形容词\n【词性槽】functional and __ design 并列形容词\n【依据】functional 与 aesthetic 是设计学经典对子\n在工业设计领域，functional（功能性）和 aesthetic（美学性）是描述产品设计的两大维\n度——前者管用，后者好看；Apple 正以\"功能+美学\"的双轨设计闻名于世，与本句精准\n契合。\n【竞争词】F) fiscal 话题完全错\nfiscal design（财政设计）不构成英文搭配，也与 functional 不在同一维度——一个讲产品属\n性、一个讲税收政策，明显风马牛不相及。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 F) —— fiscal 词性对、话题完全错。integrates functional and\n__(28)__ design 中，F fiscal 是形容词、词性匹配；陷阱在于\"functional and fiscal\"听起\n来好像在讲企业的两条线，容易让人误填。防坑：functional 是产品设计属性，应与\naesthetic 构成设计学经典对子；记住 functional/aesthetic 这对就不会误选 fiscal。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetic", "B) appropriate", "C) clusters", "D) competitive", "E) exploiting", "F) fiscal", "G) instances", "H) rehabilitated", "I) reproduced", "J) revenues", "K) safeguarding", "L) ship", "M) strategic", "N) transcend", "O) undergoing"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) ship\n动词\n【词性槽】动词不定式三个并列动作\n【依据】ship furniture 是\"运输/发货\"动词\nship 作动词义为\"发货、运输\"，与 design（设计）、manufacture（生产）构成宜家从生\n产到出货的完整产业链；ship furniture（发货家具）是英语商业语境的自然搭配。\n【竞争词】N) transcend 义不对路\ntranscend furniture（超越家具）在语义上荒谬；transcend 通常接抽象名词（limits、\nboundaries），与\"低成本生产家具\"的产业链逻辑完全脱节。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetic", "B) appropriate", "C) clusters", "D) competitive", "E) exploiting", "F) fiscal", "G) instances", "H) rehabilitated", "I) reproduced", "J) revenues", "K) safeguarding", "L) ship", "M) strategic", "N) transcend", "O) undergoing"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) reproduced\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 + 副词 + __ + 宾语，过去式谓语位\n【依据】reproduced this formula = 复制这一套打法\nreproduce a formula / model / pattern 在英语里指\"在别处复制同一套做法\"，与上文\"先\n在瑞典打造能力、再到许多其它国家\"的逻辑严丝合缝；与 overview 中\"transplant\ncapabilities wherever appropriate\"主旨完全一致。\n【竞争词】H) rehabilitated 义不对路\nrehabilitate 意为\"使（人/建筑/制度）恢复、改造\"，宾语通常是受损或失效之物；this formula\n（一套成功打法）并未受损，无需\"康复\"，逻辑不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetic", "B) appropriate", "C) clusters", "D) competitive", "E) exploiting", "F) fiscal", "G) instances", "H) rehabilitated", "I) reproduced", "J) revenues", "K) safeguarding", "L) ship", "M) strategic", "N) transcend", "O) undergoing"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) revenues\n名词\n【词性槽】by + __ 名词位\n【依据】by revenues 商业排名固定表达\nworld's Nth largest company by revenues（按营收排名世界第 N 大公司）是英美商业报\n道的高频固定句式，与\"全球电信公司排名\"语境精准契合。\n【竞争词】C) clusters 搭配错\nby clusters（按群/簇）不是衡量公司大小的维度，逻辑不通。\nE) exploiting 义不对路\nthe world's fifth largest telecom by exploiting（按开发利用排名）讲不通；E 后文有刚性搭\n配 a systematic process of exploiting, renewing and enhancing。\nK) safeguarding 义不对路\nby safeguarding（按保卫）同样不是排名维度，话题完全脱钩。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetic", "B) appropriate", "C) clusters", "D) competitive", "E) exploiting", "F) fiscal", "G) instances", "H) rehabilitated", "I) reproduced", "J) revenues", "K) safeguarding", "L) ship", "M) strategic", "N) transcend", "O) undergoing"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) undergoing\n动词\n【词性槽】were + __ 后接宾语，构成现在进行体\n【依据】undergoing reform 经历改革的标准表达\nundergo reform / undergo change 是\"经历某种变革\"的固定搭配；本段讲智利和阿根\n廷\"正在经历更快速的市场改革\"，O 在词义和搭配上同时命中。\n【竞争词】E) exploiting 主体不对\n国家\"利用\"市场改革（exploiting market reform）在语义上不通；exploiting 的主语通常是企\n业/人，且 E 在第 35 空有刚性并列结构。\nK) safeguarding 义反\nsafeguarding reform（保卫改革）与全文\"加速改革、私有化国有电信\"的语境逻辑相反——国\n家是在推进改革，不是在防卫改革。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetic", "B) appropriate", "C) clusters", "D) competitive", "E) exploiting", "F) fiscal", "G) instances", "H) rehabilitated", "I) reproduced", "J) revenues", "K) safeguarding", "L) ship", "M) strategic", "N) transcend", "O) undergoing"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) instances\n名词\n【词性槽】many other __ of … 复数名词位\n【依据】instances of failed experiments 自然搭配\ninstances of（…的实例/例子）是英语常用搭配；上一段以 Apple、IKEA、Telefonica 为正\n面例子，这里转折说\"还有许多其他（失败的）实例\"，逻辑无缝衔接。\n【竞争词】C) clusters 搭配不成立 + 语义错位\nclusters of experiments（实验簇）在英语中不是惯用搭配；clusters 通常修饰 stars、cells、\ncompanies 等，不指\"案例的合集\"。\nJ) revenues 义不对路\nrevenues of expensive failed experiments（昂贵失败实验的收入）逻辑荒谬，且 J 在第 31\n空有死搭配 by revenues。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetic", "B) appropriate", "C) clusters", "D) competitive", "E) exploiting", "F) fiscal", "G) instances", "H) rehabilitated", "I) reproduced", "J) revenues", "K) safeguarding", "L) ship", "M) strategic", "N) transcend", "O) undergoing"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) strategic\n形容词\n【词性槽】并列结构 a great deal of __ and operational\n【依据】strategic 与 operational 是经典并列\nstrategic and operational（战略与运营层面）是管理学经典对子；全文反复用 the global\nstrategist、systematic process（our research suggests that global winners typically\ncreate and sustain their international presence through a systematic process），话\n题精准锁定 strategic。\n【竞争词】A) aesthetic 话题错位\naesthetic 属设计/审美范畴，与 operational 不构成对子；本段讲的是\"持久全球优势的运营要\n求\"，不是设计美学。\nB) appropriate 义偏空泛\na great deal of appropriate（大量恰当的）与 and operational 并列在语义上失衡，且\nappropriate 在第 26 空有刚性搭配。\nF) fiscal 话题不对\nfiscal 指财政，全文未讨论财政与运营的二分，是话题外干扰。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aesthetic", "B) appropriate", "C) clusters", "D) competitive", "E) exploiting", "F) fiscal", "G) instances", "H) rehabilitated", "I) reproduced", "J) revenues", "K) safeguarding", "L) ship", "M) strategic", "N) transcend", "O) undergoing"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) exploiting\n动词\n【词性槽】of + __, renewing and enhancing 三个动名词并列\n【依据】exploiting, renewing and enhancing 进取三连\nexploit one's capabilities（开发/善用既有能力）+ renew（更新）+ enhance（增强）构\n成对核心能力的三段式进取式动作链，正对应 overview 强调的\"打造-移植-升级\"主旨；且\n与第二段 Apple 段的 exploit 用词遥相呼应。\n【竞争词】K) safeguarding 语义指向不对\nsafeguarding（保卫）属于防御性动作，与 renewing、enhancing 这两个进取性动作不在同一\n向量上；全文论调是\"主动打造并升级全球优势\"，不是\"防卫既有能力\"，K 是话题外干扰。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 K) —— safeguarding 词性形态全对、唯方向反。a systematic\nprocess of __(35)__, renewing and enhancing their core capabilities 中，K\nsafeguarding 的 -ing 形与介词 of 完全匹配，\"保卫核心能力\"中文读起来也顺；陷阱在于\n它与 renewing/enhancing 这两个进取动作方向相反。防坑：并列动词读三遍\"V1, V2,\nV3\"，看情感/方向是否同向，发现 safeguarding 一守二攻立即剔除。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "A new study found parents' aversion to certain animals would pass on to their children.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】parents+pass on 父母传给孩子\n【定位】Many of the surveyed children revealed that their parents also expressed fear or disgust of the\nsame animals. In fact, these parental emotions were strong enough to overwhelm any positive\nexperiences the children might have gained from direct experiences in nature.\n许多受访的孩子透露，他们的父母对同样这些动物也表达了恐惧或厌恶。事实上，这些父母\n的情绪强到足以盖过孩子从自然中亲身获得的任何正面体验。\n【改写】parents' aversion to certain animals ↔\ntheir parents also expressed fear or disgust of the same animals\naversion=fear or disgust 的概括\nwould pass on to their children ↔\nthere is likely a feedback loop in which an increase in people who have negative attitudes\ntowards nature in one generation will lead to a further increase in people with similar attitudes\nin the next generation\npass on=代际反馈循环的概括\n【辨邻】 D D 段 A survey of more than 5,300 school children in the Tochigi Prefecture\nexamined their perception of 14 local insect species and one spider 中只统计孩\n子，不涉及父母情绪传递，与题干 parents' aversion 缺少关键人物锚点。\nMost of the students saw the species as things to dislike or fear, or even as sources of danger\nE E 段 I have long wondered why so many of today's children react like this 仅是\nSoga 提问，父母的角色与代际传递在下一段 F 才给出，因此 E 不是 Q36 的源头。\nchildren these days avoid even harmless insects such as butterflies and dragonflies", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "The disappearance of species and ecological systems erodes our ability to keep what is left.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】species 与 ecological systems 同时消失\n【定位】Increasingly, research has shown that as species and ecosystems vanish, it also chips away at\nour ability to preserve what remains—because we no longer understand what we're losing.\n越来越多研究表明，随着物种与生态系统的消失，它也一点点削弱了我们保护剩余部分的能\n力——因为我们已经不再理解自己正在失去什么。\n【改写】The disappearance of species and ecological systems ↔ as species and ecosystems vanish\n名词化与同义替换\nerodes our ability to keep what is left ↔ chips away at our ability to preserve what remains\nerodes=chips away at；keep=preserve；what is left=what remains\n【辨邻】 G G 段 As the number of children who have biophobia increases, public interest\nand support for biodiversity conservation will gradually decline 的起点是\nbiophobia 增加，而题干 Q37 的起点是 disappearance of species and ecological\nsystems，因此 G 段不是 Q37 的源头。\ncould chip away at our societal ability to preserve what we're losing", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "A study showed that the wildest areas of Discovery Park appealed most to the public.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Discovery Park 的最野区域最吸引人\n【定位】It found that the public had the most appreciation for—and gained the most value from—the\nwildest parts of the park.\n研究发现，公众对公园中最野的部分评价最高，也从中获得了最大价值。\n【改写】the wildest areas of Discovery Park appealed most to the public ↔\nthe public had the most appreciation for—and gained the most value from—the wildest parts\nof the park\nappealed most=had the most appreciation；areas=parts\n【辨邻】 K K 段 depended on Discovery Park's relative wildness 只是说一切体验都依赖公园\n的相对原始，并不是题干 Q38 所概括的最野部分最受公众欢迎这条研究结论；研究结\n论一句话本身在 J 段。\nWe saw that a large majority of participants' interactions, especially their most meaningful\ninteractions, depended on Discovery Park's relative wildness", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "The fear of living organisms is becoming more worrisome.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】对生物的恐惧·更令人担忧\n【定位】At its worst it becomes something even more concerning: \"biophobia,\" the fear of living\nthings and a complete aversion to nature.\n最糟时它会演变成一种更令人担忧的现象：biophobia，即对生命体的恐惧和对自然的彻底\n反感。\n【改写】The fear of living organisms ↔ the fear of living things\norganisms=living things 的同义替换\nis becoming more worrisome ↔ At its worst it becomes something even more concerning\nmore worrisome=even more concerning 的程度词同义\n【辨邻】 G G 段 I believe that increased biophobia is a major, but invisible, threat to global\nbiodiversity 描述的是 biophobia 对生物多样性的威胁，而非题干 becoming more\nworrisome 所说的现象本身正变得更令人担忧——这句升级判断在 C 段。\nincreased biophobia is a major, but invisible, threat to global biodiversity", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Preventing the increase in children's fear of living creatures is also important for conserving biodiversity.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Preventing+biodiversity 防止恐惧上升与保护生物多样性并列\n【定位】Although many conservation biologists still consider that preventing the loss of wildlife\nhabitat is the most important way to conserve biodiversity, I think preventing increased\nbiophobia is also important for conservation.\n尽管许多保护生物学家仍然认为，防止野生动物栖息地的丧失是保护生物多样性最重要的途\n径，但我认为防止 biophobia 上升对保护工作同样重要。\n【改写】Preventing the increase in children's fear of living creatures ↔ preventing increased biophobia\nfear of living creatures=biophobia 的术语替换\nis also important for conserving biodiversity ↔ is also important for conservation\n并列副词 also 完全保留+词形变换\n【辨邻】 H H 段 children should experience nature more often 是给出措施，没有题干 is also\nimportant for conserving biodiversity 那种把防止恐惧上升与保护生物多样性并列\n的论点表述。\nThe authors also suggest establishing policies to guide these natural experiences and increasing\neducational programs about the natural world", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Research shows that more and deeper experience people have with nature will help save it.", "options": null, "answer": "P", "analysis": "【答案】P 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】more and deeper experiences 更多更深\n【定位】No matter what techniques we use, this growing field of research illustrates that saving nature\nrequires encouraging people to experience it more often and more deeply.\n无论我们用哪种方法，这一日益壮大的研究领域都表明，拯救自然要求鼓励人们更频繁、更\n深入地去体验自然。\n【改写】more and deeper experience people have with nature ↔\nexperience it more often and more deeply\n数量+程度并列对应\nwill help save it ↔ saving nature requires encouraging people to experience\nsaving nature=save it 的代词替换\n【辨邻】 N N 段 If we can get people to understand how much meaning and value can\ncome from having more experiences with more wild forms of nature 是 Lev 的\n展望，而题干 Research shows 指向研究领域的总结性发现——这一句出现在 P 段\nthis growing field of research illustrates。\nmore experiences with more wild forms of nature", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Though humans naturally tend to avoid dangerous animals, today's children try to stay away from even harmless ones.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】本能避开危险动物 vs 今天孩子连无害也躲\n【定位】Humans inherently avoid dangerous organisms such as bees, but children these days avoid\neven harmless insects such as butterflies and dragonflies.\n人类天生会避开像蜜蜂这样的危险生物，但今天的孩子连蝴蝶、蜻蜓这样无害的昆虫都会\n避开。\n【改写】humans naturally tend to avoid dangerous animals ↔\nHumans inherently avoid dangerous organisms such as bees\nnaturally=inherently；animals=organisms\ntoday's children try to stay away from even harmless ones ↔\nchildren these days avoid even harmless insects such as butterflies and dragonflies\nstay away from=avoid；ones=insects 的代词回指\n【辨邻】 D D 段 Most of the students saw the species as things to dislike or fear 只统计孩子\n的负面反应，并未给出题干 Q42 那种 humans naturally avoid dangerous vs\nchildren avoid harmless 的对照命题，因此不是源头。\nMost of the students saw the species as things to dislike or fear, or even as sources of danger", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Development in and around Discovery Park could cause heavy losses to the park and the local residents.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Development+Discovery Park·公园与居民两失\n【定位】If anything like that happened, both the park and the people of Seattle could lose\nsomething vital.\n如果这样的事真的发生，公园和西雅图人都会失去某种至关重要的东西。\n【改写】Development in and around Discovery Park ↔ If anything like that happened\n代词回指=概括等价\ncould cause heavy losses to the park and the local residents ↔\nboth the park and the people of Seattle could lose something vital\n双失主语+vital=heavy losses\n【辨邻】 L L 段 Most recently a plan proposed building 34 acres of much-needed\naffordable housing and parking spaces adjacent to the park 是提案描述，后果判\n断的源头是 M 段 both the park and the people of Seattle could lose something\nvital，因此 L 不是 Q43 源头。\nbringing with them noise, traffic and pollution", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "A large survey of school children found that their negative feelings grew as their experience with nature diminished.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】large survey of school children·接触越少越负面\n【定位】A survey of more than 5,300 school children in the Tochigi Prefecture examined their\nperception of 14 local insect species and one spider. The results? A collective \"ew!\" Most of\nthe students saw the species as things to dislike or fear, or even as sources of danger. The less\nexperience the students had with nature, the more negative their feelings.\n一项针对 Tochigi 县 5300 多名学童的调查考察了他们对 14 种本地昆虫和 1 种蜘蛛的看\n法。结果如何？众口一声的厌恶。大多数学生把这些物种看作讨厌、害怕，甚至危险的东\n西。与自然接触越少，他们的负面感受就越强烈。\n【改写】A large survey of school children ↔\nA survey of more than 5,300 school children in the Tochigi Prefecture\nlarge=more than 5,300 的概括\ntheir negative feelings grew as their experience with nature diminished ↔\nThe less experience the students had with nature, the more negative their feelings\nthe less…the more…=grew as diminished 的句式重构\n【辨邻】 E E 段 The results were published earlier this year in the journal Biological\nConservation 是研究发布与作者观点，并未给题干要的具体反比关系；具体反比关系\n在 D 段。\nthe study stemmed from observations about today's nature-deficient children", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Elizabeth Lev believes increased contact with more wildlife helps conserve biodiversity.", "options": null, "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Elizabeth Lev 主张更多与野生动物接触有助保护生物多样性\n【定位】If we can get people to understand how much meaning and value can come from having more\nexperiences with more wild forms of nature, then maybe we can stop this cycle and move\ntoward conserving and restoring what we have left.\n如果我们能让人们明白，更多地去体验更野生形态的自然能带来多少意义和价值，那么也许\n我们就能跳出这个循环，转而保护并恢复我们尚存的自然。\n【改写】increased contact with more wildlife ↔ more experiences with more wild forms of nature\nexperiences=contact；wild forms of nature=wildlife 的概括对应\nhelps conserve biodiversity ↔ move toward conserving and restoring what we have left\nconserve biodiversity=conserving what we have left 的概括\n【辨邻】 K K 段 depended on Discovery Park's relative wildness 给出的是体验机制；保护生\n物多样性的主张表述在 N 段 move toward conserving and restoring what we\nhave left，因此 K 不是源头。\ndepended on Discovery Park's relative wildness\nG G 段 I think preventing increased biophobia is also important for conservation\n出自 Soga，而题干 Elizabeth Lev believes 明确锁定 Lev 的主张，因此 G 不是\n源头。\nI think preventing increased biophobia is also important for conservation", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "Why do some people advocate forgiving student loan debt?", "options": ["A) They assert it will narrow the gap between the wealthy and the poor.", "B) They believe it will benefit both the economy and the underprivileged.", "C) They claim it will eliminate economic distress among college students.", "D) They think the cost of education is the responsibility of the government."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】Why do some people advocate forgiving student loan debt? 直接问 \"为何主张\"，要求\n回原文找其陈述的理由，属事实陈述题。\n【拆句】题干为标准 Why 引导的特殊疑问句，主谓宾结构清晰：主语 some people，谓语\nadvocate，宾语动名词短语 forgiving student loan debt，无从句、无否定、无陷阱。\n【定位】Some people in the US have asserted that forgiving student loan debt is one way to stimulate\nthe economy and give assistance to those in need.\n美国一些人主张：免除学生贷款债务是刺激经济、为困难人群提供援助的一种方式。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\n原文只说 give assistance to those in need，并未说 narrow the gap between the\nwealthy and the poor——\"帮助困难者\" 不等于 \"缩小贫富差距\"。\nB) ✓ 正确\nstimulate the economy ↔ benefit ... the economy\ngive assistance to those in need ↔ benefit ... the underprivileged\n预判要求同时覆盖 \"经济\" 与 \"弱势群体\"，B 用 both the economy and the\nunderprivileged 并列结构精准对应定位句的并列宾语，是唯一双满足项。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文 economically distressed 只是 \"被免债 1 万美元\" 的资格描述，并未声称免债能\neliminate economic distress among college students。\nD) 无中生有\n原文未出现 the cost of education is the responsibility of the government 这一\n表述，主张方只谈 \"免债\" 这一具体动作，未上升到 \"教育成本应由政府承担\" 的责任\n归属。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What do we learn from the Federal Reserve data?", "options": ["A) Approximately 60% of student debt remains unpaid.", "B) Cancelling student debt benefits wealthy families most.", "C) Forgiving student debt provides little benefit to universities.", "D) Low-income families owe the biggest amount of student debt."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from the Federal Reserve data? 问 \"我们从某数据得知什么\"，要求复\n述数据所支持的具体结论，属客观信息检索。\n【拆句】Wh- 疑问句，what 作宾语前置，\"learn from\" 为搭配，from 后接信息来源；句式简洁无\n修饰。\n【定位】This policy is just giving money away to universities and the most affluent students in\nattendance. Federal Reserve data reveals that the highest-income 40 percent of households\nowe approximately 60 percent of outstanding student debt, while the lowest 40 percent owe\njust under 20 percent.\n该政策不过是把钱白送给大学和在读的最富有学生。美联储数据显示：收入最高的 40% 家\n庭欠下了大约 60% 的未偿学贷，而收入最低的 40% 仅欠不到 20%。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文 60% 指 outstanding student debt 中由高收入家庭持有的比例，并非\nApproximately 60% of student debt remains unpaid——\"未偿\" 在原文里是\noutstanding（仍欠着），但 60% 修饰的是高收入家庭占比而非未偿率。\nB) ✓ 正确\ngiving money away to universities and the most affluent students in attendance ↔\nCancelling student debt benefits wealthy families most\nthe highest-income 40 percent of households owe approximately 60 percent of outstanding\nstudent debt\n↔ wealthy families\n数据要证明 \"消债是给富人送钱\"，B 把这一结论用 benefits wealthy families most 复\n述，是唯一锁定 \"受益者方向\" 的选项。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文 giving money away to universities 明示大学正是免债的受益方，C 项却说\nForgiving student debt provides little benefit to universities，与原文截然相反。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文 the lowest 40 percent owe just under 20 percent 明确说低收入家庭只欠不到\n20%，D 项称 Low-income families owe the biggest amount of student debt，方\n向完全反向。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What does the author say students are likely to do if they know they needn't repay their loans?", "options": ["A) They will choose to study subjects without considering their job prospects.", "B) They will be free to pursue their goals without being burdened financially.", "C) They will over-borrow and live beyond their means.", "D) They will be able to enroll in expensive universities."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say students are likely to do 问的是作者所言，要求复述作者在\n原文对该情形下学生行为的明确说法，属直接陈述题。\n【拆句】复合疑问句：主句 What does the author say，宾语从句 students are likely to do，\n后跟条件状语从句 if they know they needn't repay their loans；需把 if-从句视为情景\n前提。\n【定位】And, students will feel more comfortable making the irresponsible decision to go tens of\nthousands of dollars in debt to major in impractical or idealistic subjects if they know their\nloans will be forgiven.\n并且，如果学生知道自己的贷款将被免除，他们会更安心地做出不负责任的决定——为了主\n修不实用或理想化的专业而背上数万美元的债务。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nmajor in impractical or idealistic subjects ↔\nstudy subjects without considering their job prospects\nmajor in ↔ study subjects\n把 impractical / idealistic 翻译为 \"不考虑就业\" 这一行为机制，是定位句到 A 项的唯一\n等值路径；其余三项均跳出此语义域。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文将这一行为定性为 the irresponsible decision（不负责任的决定），B 项却美化为\nbe free to pursue their goals without being burdened financially（自由追求目标、\n不受经济负担），态度方向完全相反。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文谈的只是 major in impractical or idealistic subjects 选专业行为；C over-\nborrow and live beyond their means 把话题扩到 \"超支度日\" 的生活方式，越界。\nD) 无中生有\n原文未提学生因免债 enroll in expensive universities 报昂贵学校的行为，借贷增多与\n\"昂贵大学\" 没有原文支持。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What does the author imply about colleges offering online education?", "options": ["A) They cannot get away with the serious consequences.", "B) They have suffered greatly from the current pandemic.", "C) The tuition they charge is not justified by the quality of their service.", "D) The tuition they charge has surged outrageously during the pandemic."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （推断题）\n【判型】What does the author imply about colleges offering online education? 中 imply 明示\n作者并未直说，需要从其用词与反问句推断态度。\n【拆句】Wh- 疑问句嵌 about 介词短语：主干 What does the author imply，宾语 about\ncolleges offering online education，offering 为现在分词作定语；无陷阱。\n【定位】Students are paying tens of thousands of dollars per year to live at home and be lectured on\nthe Internet. Do we really want to tell colleges that they can get away with providing below-\naverage service for an outrageous cost?\n学生每年花费数万美元只为待在家里、通过网络听课。我们真的愿意告诉大学：它们可以一\n边收取离谱的费用、一边提供低于平均水平的服务，还安然无恙吗？\n【选项】A) 过度推断\n原文反问句 Do we really want to tell colleges that they can get away with ... ? 表达\n\"作者认为大学不该逃过去\"，但 A 项 They cannot get away with the serious\nconsequences 直接把 \"逃不掉\" 当作论点，作者只是质问、并未陈述其事实结果，且\n\"serious consequences\" 系原文未提的后果维度。\nB) 张冠李戴\n原文 Students are paying tens of thousands of dollars per year，受害方是学生，B\n却把 \"受害\" 戴给大学：They have suffered greatly from the current pandemic，主\n语与原文相反。\nC) ✓ 正确\nbelow-average service for an outrageous cost ↔\nThe tuition they charge is not justified by the quality of their service\noutrageous cost ↔ the tuition they charge\n反问句的反义即 \"学费不应换来低于平均的服务\"，C 用 not justified by the quality of\ntheir service 把这一态度客观化，是唯一锁定 \"价质不匹配\" 的选项。\nD) 无中生有\n原文只说学费 \"离谱\"（outrageous），未说它 has surged outrageously during the\npandemic 在大流行期间 \"飙涨\"；时间维度的涨幅描述无原文支撑。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What will happen if any of the proposed student debt plans is implemented?", "options": ["A) Plumbers and retail workers will have a chance of becoming professionals.", "B) Working-class students will have increasing access to subsidized education.", "C) Blue-collar workers will have to bear the cost of educating would-be high-earners.", "D) A growing number of students will be able to earn degrees in medicine and law."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What will happen if any of the proposed student debt plans is implemented? 问方案\n实施后的结果，原文有对应客观陈述句即可作答。\n【拆句】Wh- 疑问句加 if 条件状语：主句 What will happen，从句 if any of the proposed\nstudent debt plans is implemented，被动语态 is implemented 表 \"被实施\"；逻辑是\n\"实施→什么结果\"。\n【定位】In the case of any of these student debt plans, working-class Americans who chose not to or\ncould not afford to go to college will be subsidizing the education of the professional class.\nPlumbers and retail workers will be paying for the degrees of doctors and lawyers.\n无论这些学生贷款方案的哪一种实施，那些选择不上大学或负担不起大学的工薪阶层美国人\n都将在补贴专业阶层的教育。管道工和零售工将为医生和律师的学位埋单。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文谈的是 working-class Americans 要补贴别人，而非 \"获得成为专业阶层的机会\"；\nA Plumbers and retail workers will have a chance of becoming professionals 把方\n向反过来，让他们成为受益人。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文 working-class Americans who chose not to or could not afford to go to\ncollege 强调工薪阶层不上大学，B 说 Working-class students will have increasing\naccess to subsidized education，把不上大学的工薪阶层反向描述为 \"上大学且获\n补贴\"。\nC) ✓ 正确\nPlumbers and retail workers ↔ Blue-collar workers\nwill be paying for the degrees of doctors and lawyers ↔\nwill have to bear the cost of educating would-be high-earners\n预判要求保留 \"蓝领→白领\" 的财政方向，C 用 Blue-collar workers 与 would-be\nhigh-earners 把双方上位化后保留方向，是唯一忠实复述者。\nD) 答非所问\n原文谈的是 \"谁为这些学位埋单\" 的财政分配，而非 A growing number of students\nwill be able to earn degrees in medicine and law 这种 \"医法学位人数增加\" 的供给\n变化，未对应题干 What will happen 所问的方案后果。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What do we learn about the \"No screens under 2\" rule?", "options": ["A) It has met more and more resistance from parents.", "B) It has proved helpful to children's healthy growth.", "C) It confuses parents with regard to kids' education.", "D) It has been discarded in line with recent research."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do we learn about the \"No screens under 2\" rule? 指向一个具体规则的现\n状/命运，不含 infer/imply/mainly about，属于围绕特定对象核对事实的典型细节题。\n【拆句】题干主干 What do we learn about the \"No screens under 2\" rule，是 learn about +\n对象的常规疑问句；引号内的 No screens under 2 是省略 be 动词的口号式标题，全句结\n构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】As of today, that rule has been thrown out the window.\n从今天起，那条规则已被扔出窗外（即被彻底弃用）。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nmet more and more resistance from parents 在原文中找不到对应；rule that most\nparents cling to 反而显示家长一直紧抱旧规则，与\"抵制\"相反。\nB) 无中生有\nproved helpful to children's healthy growth 在原文中无对应；原文只是说家长一直\n奉行该规则，并未给出\"该规则被证明对孩子健康有益\"的证据。\nC) 张冠李戴\nconfuses parents with regard to kids' education 把\"令家长困惑\"扣在旧规则头上；\n但原文 in the confusing, fast-changing world of kids and media 中 confusing 形容\n的是\"kids and media 这个世界\"，不是 No screens under 2 这条规则——这是典型的\n张冠李戴。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthat rule has been thrown out the window ↔ It has been discarded\nextensively updated and revised its guidelines... to reflect new research ↔\nin line with recent research\n预判落点\"被弃用/依据新研究\"两个要素，正好分别对应 discarded 与 in line with\nrecent research，D 是定位句与紧邻背景句的整合同义替换。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What do the new AAP guidelines advocate?", "options": ["A) Young children should be accompanied by parents during screen time.", "B) Parents should be emotionally involved in their children's upbringing.", "C) Young children should watch videos carefully selected by parents.", "D) Parents should protect their children from too much media exposure."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （主旨题）\n【判型】题干 What do the new AAP guidelines advocate? 不指向某一条具体规则，而是问 new\nAAP guidelines 整体在主张什么，需要把全文若干段拼起来才能回答，属于局部主旨题。\n【拆句】题干 What do the new AAP guidelines advocate? 是 what 作宾语的常规疑问句，主语\nnew AAP guidelines、谓语 advocate；唯一需要留意 advocate 的语义指向\"主张/提倡\"，\n不是\"批评/警示\"。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nif and only if parents are watching alongside them ↔\naccompanied by parents during screen time\nCo-view with your children ↔ accompanied by parents during screen time\nthey want care-givers to take part in screen time ↔\naccompanied by parents during screen time\n预判要点\"父母陪同孩子用屏\"在 P7 P9 至少三处同义表述同时成立，A 项一句话覆盖这\n条主线，是新指南的整体主张。\nB) 过度推断\nemotionally involved in their children's upbringing 把范围从\"屏幕时间陪伴\"扩到\"整\n体育儿过程\"；本文限定话题在\"屏幕/媒体使用\"，不涉及通用育儿建议。\nC) 与原文相反\nvideos carefully selected by parents 强调父母\"挑选视频内容\"，与 shift the focus\nfrom WHAT is on the screen to WHO else is in the room 明确告示的方向相反——\n新指南要的是 WHO（有谁陪），不是 WHAT（挑什么内容）。\nD) 以偏概全\nprotect their children from too much media exposure 偏向\"控制屏幕时间总量\"，确\n实贴近 P8 与 P9 的\"一小时上限/避免独看\"，但只是 P9 的局部要点之一；它没覆盖贯穿\n全文的 co-view 主线，作为整体主张以偏概全。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What do families think of live video chat according to surveys?", "options": ["A) It should not be regarded as screen time.", "B) It helps babies to develop their verbal skills.", "C) It is not as harmful as playing games on laptops.", "D) It is a good substitute for video viewing."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do families think of live video chat according to surveys? 指向一句调查结\n论，答案落在原文一两句话里，是典型细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What do families think of live video chat according to surveys? 主干为 What do\nfamilies think of...，think of = \"对……怎么看\"；尾部 according to surveys 是状语，限定\n信息来源，不是 think 的宾语。\n【定位】Surveys indicate that families already popularly believe that \"Face time doesn't count\", or at\nleast that the benefit of virtual visits with grandparents or other relatives outweighs the\npotential cost of exposing babies to the laptop or smartphone.\n调查显示，许多家庭已经普遍相信\"视频聊天不算（屏幕时间）\"，或者至少认为与祖父母等\n亲属虚拟相见的好处大于让婴儿接触电脑或智能手机的潜在代价。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\n\"Face time doesn't count\" ↔ It should not be regarded as screen time\n预判落点是\"家庭把视频聊天排除在屏幕时间之外\"，A 恰好是这句话的书面化、完整化\n版本，是定位句的标准同义替换。\nB) 张冠李戴\n原文 P5 没有说视频聊天帮助婴儿\"发展语言能力\"；emotionally engaged 给出的也\n是\"情感投入\"而非语言；P7 谈到 learn new words 但前提是 educational media+父母\n伴看，与 live video chat 是不同话题。\nC) 无中生有\nnot as harmful as playing games on laptops 把视频聊天与\"在笔记本上玩游戏\"作比\n较，但原文 P5 只比较\"虚拟探访的好处\"与\"接触手机的潜在代价\"，从未把 video chat\n与 playing games 放一起比较。\nD) 无中生有\na good substitute for video viewing 把视频聊天说成\"看视频的好替代品\"，但 P5 只\n说家庭认为视频聊天\"不算屏幕时间\"，并未在视频聊天与看视频之间建立替代关系。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What do researchers find about kids solo viewing educational videos?", "options": ["A) It arouses their interest in language learning.", "B) It works no better than reading picture books.", "C) It hampers their development of language skills.", "D) It helps them acquire independent learning skills."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do researchers find about kids solo viewing educational videos? 指向一句\n研究结论，是典型细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What do researchers find about kids solo viewing educational videos? 主干\nWhat do researchers find about X；about 的宾语 kids solo viewing educational\nvideos 是\"动名词复合结构\"——solo viewing 作动名词中心，前面 kids 是逻辑主语，相当\n于\"孩子独自观看教育视频\"这一行为。\n【定位】The flip side of this is that many studies have actually shown poorer language skills\ncorrelated with earlier solo viewing of \"educational\" videos.\n另一方面，许多研究实际上已经表明，过早地独自观看\"教育\"视频与较差的语言能力相关。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\narouses their interest in language learning 原文未提兴趣，且原文方向是\"独看伴随\n更差语言能力\"，与\"激发兴趣\"方向相反。\nB) 偷换概念\nworks no better than reading picture books 把 treating a video or an app like a\npicture book is the best bet 错读为\"二者效果一样\"；原文是\"把视频当绘本来读才是最\n佳做法\"，并未说\"独看视频和读绘本一样\"。\nC) ✓ 正确\npoorer language skills correlated with earlier solo viewing of \"educational\" videos ↔\nIt hampers their development of language skills\n原文 poorer skills correlated with solo viewing 等同于\"独看与语言能力变差相关\"，C\n的 hampers 正是这一负向相关的动词化表达。\nD) 无中生有\nhelps them acquire independent learning skills 把\"独看\"硬扯成\"获得自主学习能\n力\"；原文不仅未提及自主学习，反而把 solo 与负向后果绑定。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author say about preschoolers ages 2 to 5?", "options": ["A) They can understand pretty well what they see on the screen.", "B) They can learn on their own without much parental guidance.", "C) They can make use of educational videos to develop digital literacy.", "D) They can relate what they learn on the screen to real life situations."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author say about preschoolers ages 2 to 5? 指向作者对一个具体\n人群的具体陈述，可在某一段内得到完整答案，是细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What does the author say about preschoolers ages 2 to 5? 主干 What does the\nauthor say about X；ages 2 to 5 是 preschoolers 的同位修饰语，等价于 who are 2 to\n5 years old，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】For preschoolers ages 2 to 5, there's more evidence that they have the ability to transfer\nknowledge from screens to the real world, including early literacy and math.\n对于 2 到 5 岁的学龄前儿童而言，有更多证据表明他们能够把从屏幕上学到的知识迁移到现\n实世界，包括早期识字与数学能力。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nunderstand pretty well what they see on the screen 与原文 help children\nunderstand what they are seeing 中的\"理解\"措辞相近，但原文这句是 AAP 对父母\n的\"建议\"——要父母帮孩子理解，并非\"孩子已经能理解\"的事实结论。\nB) 与原文相反\nlearn on their own without much parental guidance 与原文 they want care-givers\nto take part in screen time 直接相反——原文强调要父母参与，B 项说不需要父母\n指导。\nC) 偷换概念\ndevelop digital literacy 把原文 early literacy and math 中的\"早期识字\"偷换为\"数字\n素养\"——early literacy 指儿童的识字、阅读能力，与 digital literacy（数字素养/媒介\n使用能力）不是一回事。\nD) ✓ 正确\ntransfer knowledge from screens to the real world ↔\nrelate what they learn on the screen to real life situations\n预判要点\"屏上所学应用到现实\"，D 用 relate to real life situations 准确还原 transfer\nto the real world，是定位句的同义替换。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
